QUESTIONS.push(
/* ---------------- The cascade (slides 1–3) ---------------- */
{id:"L12-001", lecture:"L12", prof:"Gottlieb", tier:"new", topic:"L12", sub:"casc", skill:"recall",
 concept:"raas-sources", tags:[], source:"both",
 stem:"Where are angiotensinogen and renin produced?",
 options:[
  {t:"Angiotensinogen: liver; renin: kidney", correct:true, why:"Angiotensinogen, a very long peptide, comes from the liver; renin is made in the kidney."},
  {t:"Angiotensinogen: kidney; renin: liver", correct:false, why:"This swaps the two: the liver makes angiotensinogen and the kidney makes renin."},
  {t:"Both: adrenal cortex", correct:false, why:"The adrenal cortex releases aldosterone, the last step of the system, not angiotensinogen or renin."},
  {t:"Angiotensinogen: liver; renin: adrenal medulla", correct:false, why:"The adrenal medulla releases epinephrine and norepinephrine; renin comes from the kidney."},
  {t:"Both: liver", correct:false, why:"Only angiotensinogen comes from the liver; renin is a kidney enzyme."}],
 teach:"The renin–angiotensin–aldosterone system (RAAS) starts with angiotensinogen, a very long peptide made in the liver that floats in the blood. Renin, an enzyme made by the kidney, cuts it to angiotensin I. Angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE) then makes angiotensin II, which acts on the AT1 and AT2 receptors.",
 quote:"But normally, we should be able to produce this very long peptide called angiotensinogen, and that's going to be coming from the liver. Now our kidneys are going to be producing renin.",
 reading:[{src:"Katzung 16e, Ch. 17", sec:"Biosynthesis of Angiotensin — Angiotensinogen; Renin", t:"Angiotensinogen is the circulating protein substrate from which renin cleaves angiotensin I, and it is synthesized in the liver. Active renin in the circulation originates in the kidneys, where it is synthesized, stored and released by the granular juxtaglomerular cells."}],
 cite:"PCOL-RAAS_26s.pdf slide 3; transcript 10/8"},

{id:"L12-002", lecture:"L12", prof:"Gottlieb", tier:"new", topic:"L12", sub:"casc", skill:"recall",
 concept:"renin-substrate", tags:[], source:"both",
 stem:"What does renin do in the renin–angiotensin–aldosterone system (RAAS)?",
 options:[
  {t:"Cleaves angiotensinogen into angiotensin I", correct:true, why:"Renin cuts the large angiotensinogen peptide into the smaller angiotensin I."},
  {t:"Converts angiotensin I to angiotensin II", correct:false, why:"That is the job of angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE), the next step."},
  {t:"Breaks down bradykinin", correct:false, why:"ACE breaks down bradykinin; renin does not."},
  {t:"Stimulates aldosterone release from the adrenal cortex", correct:false, why:"Angiotensin II stimulates aldosterone release; renin acts two steps earlier."},
  {t:"Activates the AT1 receptor", correct:false, why:"Angiotensin II is the agonist at AT1; renin is an enzyme acting on angiotensinogen."}],
 teach:"Renin is an enzyme from the kidney. When it meets angiotensinogen in the blood it cuts it into a small peptide, angiotensin I. Angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE) then cuts angiotensin I into angiotensin II within milliseconds.",
 quote:"So as it comes in contact with this very large peptide full of amino acids, renin is an enzyme that is gonna cleave or cut that that amino acid into a small chunk, and now we're gonna call that angiotensin one.",
 note:"On 10/1 he said renin “is gonna cut a hormone that we make which is called angiotensin 1 into angiotensin 2”. On 10/8, teaching the RAAS slides (slides 3, 23: angiotensinogen → renin → angiotensin I → ACE → angiotensin II), he gave renin cleaving angiotensinogen into angiotensin I and ACE converting angiotensin I to angiotensin II, which matches Katzung. Keyed to 10/8 and the slides.",
 reading:[{src:"Katzung 16e, Ch. 17", sec:"Biosynthesis of Angiotensin", t:"The principal steps are the enzymatic cleavage of angiotensin I from angiotensinogen by renin, the conversion of angiotensin I to angiotensin II by converting enzyme, and the degradation of angiotensin II by peptidases. Renin is an aspartyl protease that releases the decapeptide angiotensin I from angiotensinogen."}],
 cite:"PCOL-RAAS_26s.pdf slides 3, 23; transcript 10/8"},

{id:"L12-003", lecture:"L12", prof:"Gottlieb", tier:"new", topic:"L12", sub:"casc", skill:"recall",
 concept:"ace-converts", tags:[], source:"both",
 stem:"Which statement about angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE) is CORRECT?",
 options:[
  {t:"It converts inactive angiotensin I to active angiotensin II", correct:true, why:"ACE cuts angiotensin I (inactive) into angiotensin II (active), and does so within milliseconds."},
  {t:"It cleaves angiotensinogen into angiotensin I", correct:false, why:"That step belongs to renin."},
  {t:"It converts angiotensin II into angiotensin I", correct:false, why:"The direction is reversed: angiotensin I becomes angiotensin II."},
  {t:"It makes bradykinin, a vasodilator", correct:false, why:"ACE breaks bradykinin down; it does not make it."},
  {t:"It works so slowly that angiotensin I builds up and acts on its own receptors in the vessels", correct:false, why:"Angiotensin I is converted to angiotensin II within milliseconds; angiotensin I is the inactive peptide."}],
 teach:"Angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE) cleaves angiotensin I, which is inactive, into the smaller, active angiotensin II. The conversion is so fast that angiotensin I and II were once both thought to be active. ACE also breaks down bradykinin, which matters for the ACE inhibitors.",
 quote:"For the longest time we thought that ANG1 and H2 were two active peptides, but A1 is converted to H2 like in no tomorrow, like within milliseconds. And that occurs by the angiotensin converting enzyme or the ACE enzyme.",
 cite:"PCOL-RAAS_26s.pdf slides 3, 9; transcript 10/8"},

{id:"L12-004", lecture:"L12", prof:"Gottlieb", tier:"new", topic:"L12", sub:"casc", skill:"recall",
 concept:"raas-order", tags:["exam-cue"], source:"both",
 stem:"Which sequence puts the renin–angiotensin–aldosterone system (RAAS) in the correct order, from first to last?",
 options:[
  {t:"Angiotensinogen → angiotensin I → angiotensin II → AT1", correct:true, why:"Renin makes angiotensin I from angiotensinogen, ACE makes angiotensin II, and angiotensin II acts mainly at AT1."},
  {t:"Angiotensin I → angiotensinogen → angiotensin II → AT1", correct:false, why:"Angiotensinogen is the starting peptide; angiotensin I is cut from it."},
  {t:"Angiotensinogen → angiotensin II → angiotensin I → AT1", correct:false, why:"Angiotensin I comes before angiotensin II; ACE converts I to II."},
  {t:"Angiotensinogen → angiotensin I → AT1 receptor → angiotensin II", correct:false, why:"Angiotensin II is the agonist that binds AT1, so it comes before the receptor."},
  {t:"Angiotensin II → angiotensin I → angiotensinogen → AT2", correct:false, why:"This runs the cascade backwards."}],
 teach:"The cascade: angiotensinogen (liver) is cut by renin (kidney) into angiotensin I, which angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE) converts to angiotensin II. Angiotensin II binds mainly to AT1 (Gq), which raises total peripheral resistance, Na+ and water retention and aldosterone; AT2 (Gi) opposes it. He wants the order known first to last, and how each drug changes each step.",
 quote:"And my goal is for you to have this little cascade of peptides and enzymes tattooed in your head because you're gonna need it for the exam too, OK? You should be able to tell me what comes first and what comes last, and how our drugs gonna modify their concentration or activity in our system.",
 cite:"PCOL-RAAS_26s.pdf slides 3, 23; transcript 10/8"},

{id:"L12-005", lecture:"L12", prof:"Gottlieb", tier:"new", topic:"L12", sub:"casc", skill:"recall",
 concept:"at-g-proteins", tags:[], source:"both",
 stem:"Which pairing of angiotensin II receptor and G protein is CORRECT?",
 options:[
  {t:"AT1: Gq; AT2: Gi", correct:true, why:"AT1 is Gq (more Ca++); AT2 is Gi (less cyclic AMP (cAMP)), so they act in opposite directions."},
  {t:"AT1: Gi; AT2: Gq", correct:false, why:"This swaps the two receptors."},
  {t:"AT1: Gs; AT2: Gi", correct:false, why:"AT1 is Gq, not Gs."},
  {t:"AT1: Gq; AT2: Gs", correct:false, why:"AT2 is Gi, not Gs."},
  {t:"Both AT1 and AT2: Gq", correct:false, why:"If both were Gq they could not have opposite effects; AT2 is Gi."}],
 teach:"Angiotensin II has two receptors. AT1 is coupled to Gq: more Ca++, vasoconstriction, Na+ reabsorption, remodeling. AT2 is coupled to Gi (less cAMP) and does the opposite: vasodilation, Na+ excretion, less remodeling. AT2 is the physiological antagonist of AT1.",
 quote:"Angiotensin II AT1 (Receptor, Gq) & AT2 (Receptor, Gi) … The AT2 is called to a G alphai. So what do you think it's going to do? The opposite of the AT1.",
 cite:"PCOL-RAAS_26s.pdf slides 3, 18; transcript 10/8"},

{id:"L12-006", lecture:"L12", prof:"Gottlieb", tier:"new", level:1, topic:"L12", sub:"casc", skill:"apply", multi:true,
 concept:"at1-effects", tags:["in-class"], source:"both",
 stem:"Angiotensin II binds the AT1 receptor, which is coupled to Gq. Which effects follow? Select all that apply.",
 options:[
  {t:"More Ca++ and vasoconstriction", correct:true, why:"Gq raises Ca++ in arterial and venous smooth muscle, which constricts them and raises blood pressure."},
  {t:"Less Na+ and water excretion", correct:true, why:"AT1 retains sodium, and water follows sodium, so less is excreted."},
  {t:"More aldosterone production", correct:true, why:"AT1 activation increases aldosterone, which makes more Na+ pumps and channels."},
  {t:"Less cyclic AMP (cAMP) and vasodilation", correct:false, why:"That is AT2 (Gi), the receptor that opposes AT1."},
  {t:"More Na+ excretion", correct:false, why:"Na+ excretion rises with AT2 activation, not AT1."}],
 teach:"AT1 is Gq: Ca++ rises in the smooth muscle of the arteries and veins, they constrict, and blood pressure rises. AT1 also reduces Na+ and water excretion and increases aldosterone, so volume is retained. In a bleeding patient this keeps blood going to the heart and brain.",
 quote:"And once it binds to that angiotensin type 1 receptor, and if I tell that angiotensin type 1 is called to GQ, what do you think it's going to do? Something positive or something negative. Something positive because remember GQ increases calcium, and if you increase calcium on your smooth muscle of your arteries, constrict.",
 note:"He asked the class whether AT1 activation does “something positive or something negative” and answered it himself.",
 cite:"PCOL-RAAS_26s.pdf slides 3, 18; transcript 10/8"},

{id:"L12-007", lecture:"L12", prof:"Gottlieb", tier:"new", topic:"L12", sub:"casc", skill:"tell",
 concept:"at2-physiological-antagonist", tags:[], source:"both",
 stem:"Which term describes how the AT2 receptor relates to the AT1 receptor?",
 options:[
  {t:"Physiological antagonist", correct:true, why:"AT2 acts through its own pathway (Gi) to produce the opposite effects of AT1; it does not block AT1."},
  {t:"Competitive antagonist", correct:false, why:"AT2 does not compete at AT1; both are receptors for angiotensin II."},
  {t:"Partial agonist", correct:false, why:"AT2 is a receptor, not a drug acting at AT1."},
  {t:"Inverse agonist", correct:false, why:"AT2 is not a drug acting at AT1; it is angiotensin II's second receptor."},
  {t:"Irreversible antagonist of AT1", correct:false, why:"AT2 is a separate receptor with opposite effects; it does not bind AT1."}],
 teach:"AT2 is the body's brake on angiotensin II. When angiotensin II levels get too high, AT2 (Gi) lowers blood pressure, increases Na+ and water loss and suppresses aldosterone, the opposite of AT1 (Gq). It is a physiological antagonist: opposite effects through a different receptor.",
 quote:"And our gatekeeper over here is going to be the AT2 receptor. The AT2 is called to a G alphai. So what do you think it's going to do? The opposite of the AT1. So this is our physiological antagonist.",
 cite:"PCOL-RAAS_26s.pdf slides 3, 18; transcript 10/8"},

{id:"L12-008", lecture:"L12", prof:"Gottlieb", tier:"new", topic:"L12", sub:"casc", skill:"recall",
 concept:"ang2-affinity", tags:[], source:"both",
 stem:"Angiotensin II binds mainly to which receptor?",
 options:[
  {t:"AT1", correct:true, why:"Angiotensin II has its highest affinity for AT1; about 9 out of 10 times, AT1 is what it binds."},
  {t:"AT2", correct:false, why:"Angiotensin II can bind AT2, but with much lower affinity; AT2 is the physiological antagonist."},
  {t:"Mineralocorticoid receptor (MR)", correct:false, why:"The MR is aldosterone's nuclear receptor."},
  {t:"β1 adrenergic receptor", correct:false, why:"β1 on the juxtaglomerular cells controls renin release; angiotensin II does not bind it."}],
 teach:"Angiotensin II can bind AT1 or AT2, but its affinity is highest for AT1, so AT1 effects (constriction, Na+ retention, aldosterone, remodeling) dominate. Activating AT2 takes much more angiotensin II, which is why blocking AT1 with an ARB (angiotensin II receptor blocker) shifts angiotensin II onto AT2.",
 quote:"It has the highest affinity for the AT1 receptor. So what I'd like for you to do is to make that arrow going to the AT1, the ancient tensin type 1 receptor, as bold as you can because that's 9 out of 10, that's who it's gonna be binding to, OK?",
 note:"Katzung (Ch. 17, Angiotensin Receptors) states that angiotensin II binds equally to both subtypes, with AT1 predominating in vascular smooth muscle. On 10/8 he taught that angiotensin II has its highest affinity for AT1 and needs a much higher concentration to activate AT2. Keyed to the lecture.",
 reading:[{src:"Katzung 16e, Ch. 17", sec:"Angiotensin Receptors & Mechanism of Action", t:"Two angiotensin II receptor subtypes, AT1 and AT2, are G protein–coupled. Most known actions of angiotensin II are mediated by AT1, a Gq-coupled receptor that activates phospholipase C and leads to smooth muscle contraction; AT1 predominates in vascular smooth muscle. AT2 stimulation causes vasodilation that may counteract AT1 vasoconstriction."}],
 cite:"PCOL-RAAS_26s.pdf slide 3; transcript 10/8"},

{id:"L12-009", lecture:"L12", prof:"Gottlieb", tier:"new", topic:"L12", sub:"casc", skill:"apply",
 concept:"hemorrhage-renin", tags:["in-class"], source:"both",
 stem:"A person cuts a finger while cooking and loses a large volume of blood. Which response is most likely?",
 options:[
  {t:"Sympathetic activity and renin release rise", correct:true, why:"Falling blood pressure is sensed by the baroreceptors; sympathetic activity to the kidney rises and renin is produced."},
  {t:"Renin release falls as blood pressure rises", correct:false, why:"Losing volume lowers blood pressure, it does not raise it."},
  {t:"AT2 activation dominates and vessels dilate", correct:false, why:"In a stress situation the system works through AT1: constriction and Na+ retention."},
  {t:"Aldosterone falls, so more Na+ is excreted", correct:false, why:"Aldosterone rises to retain Na+ and water and restore volume."},
  {t:"Parasympathetic activity to the kidney rises", correct:false, why:"The kidney is controlled by the sympathetic system; its rise drives renin."}],
 teach:"Bleeding lowers blood volume and blood pressure. The baroreceptors sense less blood returning to the heart and increase sympathetic output; sympathetic activity to the kidney produces renin. Angiotensin II then constricts vessels, keeps blood going to the heart and brain (the skin goes pale and cold), and retains Na+ and water.",
 quote:"So as my blood pressure goes down, who's gonna sense that I don't have enough blood returning to the heart? The better receptors and that's gonna increase the sympathetics. As I increase the sympathetics to the renal system, to the kidneys, that's gonna lead to the pro the production of renin, OK?",
 note:"He asked the class whether blood pressure goes up or down when a person is hemorrhaging, then walked through the response. “The better receptors” is the transcription of “the baroreceptors”.",
 cite:"PCOL-RAAS_26s.pdf slide 3; transcript 10/8"},

{id:"L12-010", lecture:"L12", prof:"Gottlieb", tier:"new", topic:"L12", sub:"casc", skill:"recall",
 concept:"raas-normally-off", tags:[], source:"transcript",
 stem:"Which statement about the renin–angiotensin–aldosterone system (RAAS) is CORRECT?",
 options:[
  {t:"It should be on only under certain circumstances", correct:true, why:"It is designed for stressful situations such as blood loss; it is not supposed to be on all the time."},
  {t:"It is meant to be fully active at all times, even at rest", correct:false, why:"It is normally on only under certain circumstances."},
  {t:"Its activation lowers blood pressure", correct:false, why:"Through AT1 it constricts vessels and retains volume, raising blood pressure."},
  {t:"Diet and genetics cannot raise its activity", correct:false, why:"Diet or genetics can keep it elevated in some patients, causing high blood pressure."},
  {t:"No drugs act on it", correct:false, why:"Many drugs counteract it: renin inhibitors, angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors, angiotensin II receptor blockers, mineralocorticoid receptor antagonists and β blockers."}],
 teach:"The system is built to keep a person alive in a crisis such as bleeding and is normally on only under certain circumstances. In some patients, diet or genetics keep it elevated, which causes high blood pressure; drugs that block it are used to counteract that.",
 quote:"So we usually don't, we're usually not supposed to have the system on. All the time we're only supposed to have it on under certain circumstances. However, some patients either because of their diet or because of their genetics may have a higher levels of the system elevated and they're going to have high blood pressure.",
 cite:"PCOL-RAAS_26s.pdf slide 3; transcript 10/8"},

/* ---------------- Drug targets (slides 4, 23–25) ---------------- */
{id:"L12-011", lecture:"L12", prof:"Gottlieb", tier:"new", topic:"L12", sub:"targets", skill:"tell",
 concept:"drug-target-pairs", tags:[], source:"both",
 stem:"Which pairing of drug and target in the renin–angiotensin–aldosterone system (RAAS) is CORRECT?",
 options:[
  {t:"Aliskiren: renin", correct:true, why:"Aliskiren is the renin inhibitor (Ri)."},
  {t:"Captopril: AT1 receptor", correct:false, why:"Captopril, a “-pril”, inhibits angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE)."},
  {t:"Losartan: angiotensin-converting enzyme", correct:false, why:"Losartan, a “-sartan”, blocks the AT1 receptor."},
  {t:"Spironolactone: renin", correct:false, why:"Spironolactone blocks the mineralocorticoid (aldosterone) receptor at the end of the cascade."},
  {t:"Metoprolol: mineralocorticoid receptor", correct:false, why:"Metoprolol blocks β1, which lowers renin production."}],
 teach:"The five drugs on his review slide (Captopril, Losartan, Aliskiren, Metoprolol, Spironolactone) each hit one point: aliskiren blocks renin; captopril blocks angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE); losartan blocks AT1; metoprolol blocks β1 on the juxtaglomerular cells so less renin is made; spironolactone blocks the mineralocorticoid receptor (MR) for aldosterone.",
 quote:"How is that oops, how is that captopril affects the brain, the rest system? That's gonna be our ACE inhibitor. How is losartan? That's gonna be our 81 antagonist. What about Taliarin, the renin inhibitor? Metoprolol is our selective beta one antagonist, but all beta blockers block who beta one, so they all can lower the renin production.",
 cite:"PCOL-RAAS_26s.pdf slides 4, 23–25; transcript 10/8"},

{id:"L12-012", lecture:"L12", prof:"Gottlieb", tier:"new", topic:"L12", sub:"targets", skill:"recall",
 concept:"sartan-suffix", tags:[], source:"both",
 stem:"A drug's generic name ends in “-sartan”. What is its mechanism of action?",
 options:[
  {t:"It blocks the AT1 receptor", correct:true, why:"Any “-sartan” is an angiotensin II receptor blocker (ARB): it binds and inhibits AT1."},
  {t:"It inhibits angiotensin-converting enzyme", correct:false, why:"That is the “-prils”."},
  {t:"It inhibits renin", correct:false, why:"That is aliskiren."},
  {t:"It blocks the mineralocorticoid receptor", correct:false, why:"That is spironolactone and eplerenone."},
  {t:"It blocks β1 receptors", correct:false, why:"That is metoprolol and the other β blockers (“-olol”)."}],
 teach:"Losartan, valsartan, olmesartan and the other “-sartans” are angiotensin II receptor blockers (ARBs). They bind directly to the AT1 receptor and prevent angiotensin II from binding. Knowing one means knowing them all.",
 quote:"Those are going to be losartans, losartan, and losartan, valsartan. It doesn't matter what sartan you have. So as long as you know one, you know them all, and those drugs are directly binding and inhibiting the AT1 receptor and thus preventing the binding of angiotensin 2.",
 cite:"PCOL-RAAS_26s.pdf slides 4, 13; transcript 10/8"},

{id:"L12-013", lecture:"L12", prof:"Gottlieb", tier:"new", topic:"L12", sub:"targets", skill:"recall",
 concept:"pril-suffix", tags:[], source:"both",
 stem:"Which of these drugs is an angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE) inhibitor?",
 options:[
  {t:"Quinapril", correct:true, why:"Every “-pril” is an ACE inhibitor."},
  {t:"Valsartan", correct:false, why:"A “-sartan”: AT1 receptor blocker."},
  {t:"Aliskiren", correct:false, why:"The renin inhibitor."},
  {t:"Eplerenone", correct:false, why:"A mineralocorticoid receptor antagonist."},
  {t:"Metoprolol", correct:false, why:"A selective β1 antagonist."}],
 teach:"The ACE inhibitors all end in “-pril”: captopril, enalapril, lisinopril, benazepril, quinapril, ramipril and others. Whichever “-pril” it is, it blocks angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE) and lowers angiotensin II.",
 quote:"This is going to be also a very easy drug to pick up because other than the month of April, every one of them is going to finish April, right? It's enalapril. Captopril, lisinopril, quinapril, so it doesn't matter what pril you have, that's gonna be an ACE inhibitor.",
 cite:"PCOL-RAAS_26s.pdf slides 4, 11; transcript 10/8"},

{id:"L12-014", lecture:"L12", prof:"Gottlieb", tier:"new", topic:"L12", sub:"targets", skill:"recall", multi:true,
 concept:"spironolactone-classes", tags:[], source:"both",
 stem:"Spironolactone can be placed in which drug classes? Select all that apply.",
 options:[
  {t:"Aldosterone antagonist", correct:true, why:"It blocks aldosterone's receptor."},
  {t:"Mineralocorticoid receptor (MR) antagonist", correct:true, why:"The MR is the receptor aldosterone binds."},
  {t:"Potassium-sparing diuretic", correct:true, why:"Blocking aldosterone loses Na+ and spares K+."},
  {t:"Renin inhibitor", correct:false, why:"That is aliskiren."},
  {t:"Angiotensin II receptor blocker (ARB)", correct:false, why:"That is losartan and the other “-sartans”."}],
 teach:"Spironolactone carries three class names depending on who describes it: aldosterone antagonist, mineralocorticoid receptor (MR) antagonist, and potassium-sparing diuretic. All three describe one action: blocking aldosterone at the MR, so Na+ is lost and K+ is kept.",
 quote:"And then we have over here spironolactone, which is our aldosterone antagonist, also known as MR antagonist or potassium sparing diuretics. So one drug has 3 classes that you can put them into it, depending on who is describing it, OK?",
 cite:"PCOL-RAAS_26s.pdf slides 21, 24–25; transcript 10/8"},

/* ---------------- Renin and its release (slides 5–7) ---------------- */
{id:"L12-015", lecture:"L12", prof:"Gottlieb", tier:"new", topic:"L12", sub:"renin", skill:"recall",
 concept:"renin-site", tags:[], source:"both",
 stem:"Which cells synthesize, store and secrete renin?",
 options:[
  {t:"Granular juxtaglomerular cells", correct:true, why:"Renin is made, stored in granules and secreted by exocytosis from the granular juxtaglomerular cells of the renal afferent arteriole."},
  {t:"Liver cells", correct:false, why:"The liver makes angiotensinogen, the substrate of renin."},
  {t:"Adrenal cortex cells", correct:false, why:"The adrenal cortex releases aldosterone."},
  {t:"Adrenal medulla cells", correct:false, why:"The adrenal medulla releases epinephrine and norepinephrine."},
  {t:"Collecting duct cells of the nephron", correct:false, why:"Collecting duct cells are where aldosterone makes more Na+ pumps and channels."}],
 teach:"Renin comes from the granular juxtaglomerular cells in the wall of the renal afferent arteriole: they synthesize it, store it in granules and secrete it by exocytosis. Its release is controlled locally (macula densa, intrarenal baroreceptor) and by the central nervous system through β1 receptors.",
 quote:"Granular Juxtaglomerular Cells … Renal Afferent Arteriole … Synthesis, Storage (Granules), and Secretion (Exocytosis) … Now our kidneys are going to be producing renin.",
 reading:[{src:"Katzung 16e, Ch. 17", sec:"Biosynthesis of Angiotensin — Renin", t:"Within the kidney, renin is synthesized and stored in the juxtaglomerular apparatus of the nephron. Specialized granular cells called juxtaglomerular cells are the site of its synthesis, storage and release, and active renin is released by exocytosis when the juxtaglomerular apparatus is stimulated."}],
 cite:"PCOL-RAAS_26s.pdf slide 5; transcript 10/8"},

{id:"L12-016", lecture:"L12", prof:"Gottlieb", tier:"new", topic:"L12", sub:"renin", skill:"recall", multi:true,
 concept:"renin-release-triggers", tags:[], source:"both",
 stem:"Which changes increase renin release? Select all that apply.",
 options:[
  {t:"Low Na+ Cl− sensed at the macula densa", correct:true, why:"Low sodium means the system must retain more, so renin goes up (inverse relationship)."},
  {t:"Low blood pressure reaching the kidney", correct:true, why:"The intrarenal baroreceptors raise renin when renal perfusion falls (inverse relationship)."},
  {t:"More sympathetic activity at β1", correct:true, why:"β1 on the juxtaglomerular cells is Gs and stimulates renin (direct relationship)."},
  {t:"High Na+ Cl− sensed at the macula densa", correct:false, why:"With enough sodium, renin is shut off."},
  {t:"High blood pressure reaching the kidney", correct:false, why:"High renal pressure lowers renin."},
  {t:"Blockade of β1 by metoprolol", correct:false, why:"β1 blockade lowers renin production."}],
 teach:"Three controls set renin release. Two act within the kidney: the macula densa pathway (it senses Na+ Cl−; low sodium → more renin) and the intrarenal baroreceptor pathway (low pressure → more renin). The third acts through the central nervous system: sympathetic β1 (Gs) on the juxtaglomerular cells (more sympathetic → more renin). That is one direct and two inverse relationships.",
 quote:"So what is going to be our relationship if it's sodium. It's slow, we're gonna make more running. If my pressure is low, I'm gonna make more rain. And if my sympathetic is high, I'm going to make more money. So we have one direct. Proportional and two inverse proportional relationships, right?",
 note:"“Running”, “rain” and “money” are the transcription of “renin”.",
 reading:[{src:"Katzung 16e, Ch. 17", sec:"Control of Renin Release", t:"The macula densa signals the juxtaglomerular cells so that there is an inverse relationship between NaCl delivery and renin release, and the renal vascular baroreceptor gives an inverse relationship between renal artery pressure and renin release. Norepinephrine from renal sympathetic nerves stimulates renin release, directly through β1 adrenoceptors on the juxtaglomerular cells, so reflex sympathetic activation by hypotension or hypovolemia activates the system."}],
 cite:"PCOL-RAAS_26s.pdf slides 5–7; transcript 10/8"},

{id:"L12-017", lecture:"L12", prof:"Gottlieb", tier:"new", topic:"L12", sub:"renin", skill:"recall",
 concept:"beta1-renin", tags:[], source:"both",
 stem:"Through which receptor on the juxtaglomerular cells does the sympathetic nervous system stimulate renin release?",
 options:[
  {t:"β1 (Gs)", correct:true, why:"β1 receptors on the juxtaglomerular cells are Gs and stimulate renin production."},
  {t:"α1 (Gq)", correct:false, why:"α1 constricts blood vessels; the renin pathway on the slide is β1."},
  {t:"α2 (Gi)", correct:false, why:"α2 is inhibitory and suppresses sympathetic outflow."},
  {t:"β2 (Gs)", correct:false, why:"β2 relaxes smooth muscle; the renin pathway is β1."},
  {t:"M3 (Gq)", correct:false, why:"The kidney has sympathetic innervation; M3 is not the renin pathway."}],
 teach:"The third control of renin release acts through the central nervous system: sympathetic nerves release norepinephrine onto β1 receptors (Gs) on the juxtaglomerular cells, stimulating renin production. This is why β blockers such as metoprolol lower renin.",
 quote:"And finally, as we alluded to that yesterday with adrenergic lectures, the beta ones, they are GS, they're going to stimulate the production of renin and thus causing us to produce more renin.",
 reading:[{src:"Katzung 16e, Ch. 17", sec:"Control of Renin Release — Sympathetic Nervous System", t:"Norepinephrine released from renal sympathetic nerves stimulates renin release indirectly through the baroreceptor and macula densa mechanisms and directly by an action on the juxtaglomerular cells. In humans the direct effect is mediated by β1 adrenoceptors."}],
 cite:"PCOL-RAAS_26s.pdf slides 5, 7; transcript 10/8"},

{id:"L12-018", lecture:"L12", prof:"Gottlieb", tier:"new", topic:"L12", sub:"renin", skill:"apply",
 concept:"macula-densa-low-na", tags:["in-class"], source:"both",
 stem:"The macula densa senses that very little Na+ Cl− is being absorbed. What happens to renin secretion?",
 options:[
  {t:"Renin secretion increases", correct:true, why:"The system's purpose is to retain sodium, so low sodium turns renin up."},
  {t:"Renin secretion decreases", correct:false, why:"Renin falls when sodium is high, not low."},
  {t:"Renin secretion stops", correct:false, why:"Renin is shut off when there is already enough sodium."},
  {t:"Renin secretion is unchanged", correct:false, why:"The macula densa is one of the three controls of renin release, so it changes renin."}],
 teach:"The macula densa “tastes” how much Na+ Cl− is being absorbed. Because the system exists to retain sodium, low sodium raises renin production and secretion, and high sodium shuts it off: an inverse relationship.",
 quote:"So if you have low sodium, what do you think is going to happen to the production and secretion of renmin? Is it gonna go up or down? If I have low sodium. And the purpose of this system is to retain sodium. I'm gonna increase the production r because it's gonna try to get more back in that I'm not getting through the process.",
 note:"He asked the class whether renin goes up or down with low sodium, then answered.",
 cite:"PCOL-RAAS_26s.pdf slides 5–6; transcript 10/8"},

{id:"L12-019", lecture:"L12", prof:"Gottlieb", tier:"new", level:1, topic:"L12", sub:"renin", skill:"apply",
 concept:"diuretic-compensation", tags:[], source:"transcript",
 stem:"A patient taking a diuretic loses Na+ in the urine. Which compensation is most likely?",
 options:[
  {t:"More renin and more Na+ retention", correct:true, why:"Losing sodium lowers what the macula densa senses, so the RAAS is turned up to retain sodium."},
  {t:"Less renin and more Na+ excretion", correct:false, why:"Low sodium raises renin; it does not lower it."},
  {t:"Less aldosterone production", correct:false, why:"More renin leads to more angiotensin II and aldosterone."},
  {t:"Less AT1 activation", correct:false, why:"More renin leads to more angiotensin II at AT1."}],
 teach:"Diuretics work by making the patient lose sodium. The body compensates through the renin–angiotensin–aldosterone system (RAAS), retaining more sodium, which is why a RAAS drug is eventually added to a diuretic patient's therapy.",
 quote:"One of the problems that we have with diuretics is because diuretics works by making you losing sodium. And then you compensate by retaining more sodium to the RAS system. So eventually we have to add a RAS drug to a diuretic patient because they are compensating for that process.",
 cite:"PCOL-RAAS_26s.pdf slide 6; transcript 10/8"},

/* ---------------- Aliskiren (slide 8) ---------------- */
{id:"L12-020", lecture:"L12", prof:"Gottlieb", tier:"new", topic:"L12", sub:"alisk", skill:"recall",
 concept:"aliskiren-moa", tags:[], source:"both",
 stem:"Which describes the mechanism of action of aliskiren?",
 options:[
  {t:"Reversible renin inhibitor", correct:true, why:"Aliskiren binds plasma renin with high affinity and is a reversible antagonist of the enzyme."},
  {t:"Irreversible renin inhibitor", correct:false, why:"The slide marks aliskiren as a reversible antagonist."},
  {t:"Reversible angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitor", correct:false, why:"That is the “-prils”."},
  {t:"AT1 receptor antagonist", correct:false, why:"That is the “-sartans”."},
  {t:"β1 antagonist on juxtaglomerular cells", correct:false, why:"That is metoprolol, which lowers renin release rather than blocking renin."}],
 teach:"Aliskiren is a renin inhibitor: it binds plasma renin with high affinity and blocks it reversibly. Its name helps: a big A at the start and “-ren” at the end, a renin antagonist. Blocking renin reduces plasma renin activity (PRA) and the levels of angiotensin I, angiotensin II and aldosterone.",
 quote:"The first one over here is gonna be alicain, and this is gonna be easier for you to remember because you tell it's an antagonist to the renin enzyme, right? You have a big A right at the beginning and you have a RN at the end. So it's a renin antagonist. … So we can predict that this drug can reversibly inhibit that rest system.",
 reading:[{src:"Katzung 16e, Ch. 17", sec:"Inhibition of the Renin-Angiotensin System — Renin Inhibitors", t:"Cleavage of angiotensinogen by renin is the rate-limiting step in forming angiotensin II and so is a logical target. Aliskiren, the first nonpeptide renin inhibitor approved for hypertension, produces dose-dependent reductions in plasma renin activity and in angiotensin I, angiotensin II and aldosterone."}],
 cite:"PCOL-RAAS_26s.pdf slides 4, 8; transcript 10/8"},

{id:"L12-021", lecture:"L12", prof:"Gottlieb", tier:"new", topic:"L12", sub:"alisk", skill:"recall",
 concept:"rate-limiting-enzyme", tags:["in-class"], source:"both",
 stem:"Renin is the rate-limiting enzyme of the renin–angiotensin–aldosterone system (RAAS). What follows from blocking it?",
 options:[
  {t:"Every step downstream of renin goes down", correct:true, why:"Renin is the first crucial step; without it there is no angiotensin I, no angiotensin II and no receptor activation."},
  {t:"Only angiotensin II goes down", correct:false, why:"Angiotensin I, angiotensin II, AT1 and AT2 activation and aldosterone all go down."},
  {t:"Angiotensin I builds up", correct:false, why:"Angiotensin I builds up with angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE) inhibitors, which block one step later; with renin blocked it falls."},
  {t:"Angiotensinogen production falls", correct:false, why:"Angiotensinogen is upstream; the body tries to make more of it to compensate."},
  {t:"AT1 is blocked while AT2 activation rises", correct:false, why:"That is the angiotensin II receptor blocker (ARB) pattern; with renin blocked, both receptors see less angiotensin II."}],
 teach:"A rate-limiting enzyme is the first crucial step of a process. Block renin and everything downstream goes down (angiotensin I, angiotensin II, AT1 and AT2 activation, aldosterone), while what is upstream (angiotensinogen, renin) goes up as the body compensates.",
 quote:"So the rate limit enzyme is the first one, right? So if you block that, everything downstream from that goes down because you're missing the first crucial step to make the processes. There's no way around",
 note:"He asked the class what it means to be the rate-limiting enzyme; a student answered “You run out of it. If you run out of it, no reaction” and he added that it is the first step.",
 cite:"PCOL-RAAS_26s.pdf slide 8; transcript 10/8"},

{id:"L12-022", lecture:"L12", prof:"Gottlieb", tier:"new", level:1, topic:"L12", sub:"alisk", skill:"apply", multi:true,
 concept:"aliskiren-effects", tags:[], source:"both",
 stem:"A patient starts aliskiren. Which changes are expected? Select all that apply.",
 options:[
  {t:"Angiotensin I falls", correct:true, why:"Without renin, little angiotensin I is cut from angiotensinogen."},
  {t:"Angiotensin II falls", correct:true, why:"Less angiotensin I means less angiotensin II."},
  {t:"Aldosterone falls", correct:true, why:"Less angiotensin II at AT1 means less aldosterone."},
  {t:"Na+ and water excretion rises", correct:true, why:"Less retention means more Na+ and water is lost."},
  {t:"AT2 activation rises", correct:false, why:"With less angiotensin II, AT2 activation also goes down; a rise in AT2 activation is the angiotensin II receptor blocker (ARB) pattern."},
  {t:"Angiotensin I builds up", correct:false, why:"That happens with angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE) inhibitors, which block the step after angiotensin I."}],
 teach:"Aliskiren blocks renin, the first step, so everything downstream falls: angiotensin I, angiotensin II, AT1 and AT2 activation, total peripheral resistance (TPR) and aldosterone, and Na+ and water excretion rises. Only what is upstream (angiotensinogen, renin) goes up as the body compensates.",
 quote:"So I know that NG1 is going down, NG2 is going down, activation of A1 and A2 is gonna go down. Blood pressure goes down to your TPR, your SVR, right, uh, sodium and water retention is going to go down, so you're going to lose more, so you're going to increase the excretion. Aldosterone is going to go down.",
 reading:[{src:"Katzung 16e, Ch. 17", sec:"Renin Inhibitors", t:"In healthy subjects aliskiren produces dose-dependent reductions in plasma renin activity and in plasma angiotensin I, angiotensin II and aldosterone concentrations. It does not increase bradykinin levels."}],
 cite:"PCOL-RAAS_26s.pdf slides 8, 28; transcript 10/8"},

{id:"L12-023", lecture:"L12", prof:"Gottlieb", tier:"new", topic:"L12", sub:"alisk", skill:"recall",
 concept:"aliskiren-bioavailability", tags:[], source:"transcript",
 stem:"Aliskiren's oral bioavailability is only about 2 to 3%. Why is it still effective?",
 options:[
  {t:"Its affinity for renin is very high", correct:true, why:"It binds renin so tightly that a little drug is enough."},
  {t:"It binds renin irreversibly", correct:false, why:"Aliskiren is a reversible antagonist."},
  {t:"It also blocks AT1 receptors", correct:false, why:"AT1 block is the angiotensin II receptor blockers (ARBs); aliskiren acts on renin."},
  {t:"It also inhibits angiotensin-converting enzyme", correct:false, why:"That is the ACE inhibitors."},
  {t:"Its metabolite is more potent", correct:false, why:"An active metabolite with greater affinity (EXP 3174) belongs to losartan."}],
 teach:"Aliskiren has a low bioavailability, about 2 to 3%, but it has such a high affinity for renin that only a little drug is needed. It is the newest of the renin–angiotensin–aldosterone system (RAAS) drugs, more expensive, and has less data behind it than the ACE inhibitors.",
 quote:"Alicekin has a bioavailability of about like 2 to 3%. That's pretty low. Would you agree with me? But that's so potent, it has such a high affinity for the renin enzyme, you don't need a lot. You just need a little sprinkle.",
 cite:"PCOL-RAAS_26s.pdf slide 8; transcript 10/8"},

{id:"L12-024", lecture:"L12", prof:"Gottlieb", tier:"new", topic:"L12", sub:"alisk", skill:"apply",
 concept:"start-low-go-slow", tags:[], source:"transcript",
 stem:"Which statement about starting a patient on aliskiren is CORRECT?",
 options:[
  {t:"Start at a low dose and titrate up", correct:true, why:"If renin is driving the hypertension, a high first dose can drop the pressure too far; start low and go slow."},
  {t:"Start at the highest dose", correct:false, why:"A high first dose can make blood pressure drop too far."},
  {t:"Angiotensinogen production falls", correct:false, why:"The body compensates by making more angiotensinogen and renin."},
  {t:"Renin levels are measured before each start", correct:false, why:"Typically no test of renin levels is done."},
  {t:"Blood pressure cannot fall too far with this drug", correct:false, why:"When renin drives the blood pressure, blocking it can make the pressure drop sharply."}],
 teach:"Blocking renin makes the body compensate upstream, making more angiotensinogen and renin, so the dose may need to rise. Renin levels are not usually tested, and if renin is what drives a patient's hypertension, a high first dose can make the pressure drop sharply. So therapy starts low and is titrated slowly, as with a quantal dose–response curve.",
 quote:"Well, your body can try to compensate by making more of the liver enzyme, the huge angiotensinogen, and trying to make more of the renin. So what do we do? We increase the dose. So we're gonna start with the lowest dose and we're gonna go slowly titrating to the level that we want them to be. Because if you start with the highest dose, you can just tank their blood pressure.",
 cite:"PCOL-RAAS_26s.pdf slide 8; transcript 10/8"},

/* ---------------- ACE inhibitors (slides 9–12) ---------------- */
{id:"L12-025", lecture:"L12", prof:"Gottlieb", tier:"new", topic:"L12", sub:"acei", skill:"recall",
 concept:"acei-primary-moa", tags:[], source:"both",
 stem:"What is the primary mechanism of action of angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE) inhibitors?",
 options:[
  {t:"Block conversion of angiotensin I to II", correct:true, why:"They inhibit ACE reversibly and selectively, so less angiotensin II is made."},
  {t:"Block cleavage of angiotensinogen by renin", correct:false, why:"That is aliskiren."},
  {t:"Block angiotensin II at the AT1 receptor", correct:false, why:"That is the angiotensin II receptor blockers (ARBs) (“-sartans”)."},
  {t:"Block aldosterone at the mineralocorticoid receptor", correct:false, why:"That is spironolactone and eplerenone."},
  {t:"Speed the breakdown of bradykinin", correct:false, why:"ACE breaks bradykinin down; blocking ACE raises bradykinin."}],
 teach:"All the ACE inhibitors share one primary mechanism: they selectively and reversibly inhibit angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE), so angiotensin I (inactive) is not converted to angiotensin II (active). With less angiotensin II, total peripheral resistance, Na+ and water retention and aldosterone all go down. The secondary mechanism is more bradykinin.",
 quote:"So the mechanism of action of all the ACE inhibitors, so you just need to know one is gonna be to inhibit the conversion of N1 into H2, OK, so that's gonna be the primary.",
 reading:[{src:"Katzung 16e, Ch. 17", sec:"Angiotensin-Converting Enzyme Inhibitors", t:"Orally active ACE inhibitors are directed against the active site of the enzyme; captopril and enalapril are examples. They differ in structure and pharmacokinetics but are interchangeable in clinical use, and they decrease blood pressure and promote natriuresis."}],
 cite:"PCOL-RAAS_26s.pdf slides 9–10, 12; transcript 10/8"},

{id:"L12-026", lecture:"L12", prof:"Gottlieb", tier:"new", topic:"L12", sub:"acei", skill:"recall", multi:true,
 concept:"acei-two-mechanisms", tags:["exam-cue"], source:"both",
 stem:"By which mechanisms do angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE) inhibitors lower blood pressure? Select all that apply.",
 options:[
  {t:"Less angiotensin II is made", correct:true, why:"Primary mechanism: ACE is blocked, so angiotensin I is not converted to angiotensin II."},
  {t:"Less bradykinin is broken down", correct:true, why:"Secondary mechanism: ACE also breaks down bradykinin, a potent vasodilator, so bradykinin rises."},
  {t:"AT2 activation increases", correct:false, why:"With less angiotensin II, AT2 activation falls; more AT2 activation is the angiotensin II receptor blocker (ARB) pattern."},
  {t:"Renin is inhibited directly", correct:false, why:"That is aliskiren."},
  {t:"β1 on juxtaglomerular cells is blocked", correct:false, why:"That is metoprolol and the other β blockers."}],
 teach:"ACE has two jobs: converting angiotensin I to angiotensin II, and breaking down bradykinin. ACE inhibitors therefore lower the constrictor (angiotensin II) and raise a potent vasodilator (bradykinin). Bradykinin also raises prostaglandins and is behind the dry cough and angioedema.",
 quote:"So primary mechanism of action, block the ACE enzyme, secondary, increase levels of bradykinin, which is the potent uh vasodilator. This makes a fantastic selectto question because it gives me more than one right answer to ask you, right?",
 reading:[{src:"Katzung 16e, Ch. 17", sec:"Angiotensin-Converting Enzyme Inhibitors", t:"ACE inhibitors block the conversion of angiotensin I to angiotensin II and also inhibit the degradation of bradykinin. Inhibiting bradykinin metabolism contributes significantly to their hypotensive action, and bradykinin is apparently responsible for some adverse effects, including cough and angioedema."}],
 cite:"PCOL-RAAS_26s.pdf slides 9, 12; transcript 10/8"},

{id:"L12-028", lecture:"L12", prof:"Gottlieb", tier:"new", level:1, topic:"L12", sub:"acei", skill:"apply", multi:true,
 concept:"acei-up-down", tags:[], source:"both",
 stem:"Which changes are expected with an angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE) inhibitor? Select all that apply.",
 options:[
  {t:"Angiotensin I builds up", correct:true, why:"Angiotensin I is upstream of the blocked enzyme and has nowhere to go."},
  {t:"Bradykinin rises", correct:true, why:"ACE no longer breaks bradykinin down."},
  {t:"AT1 activation falls", correct:true, why:"Without angiotensin II, the agonist, AT1 is not activated."},
  {t:"AT2 activation falls", correct:true, why:"AT2 also loses its agonist, angiotensin II."},
  {t:"Angiotensin II rises", correct:false, why:"Angiotensin II is downstream of the block and falls."},
  {t:"AT2 activation rises", correct:false, why:"That is the angiotensin II receptor blocker (ARB) pattern, where angiotensin II is shifted onto AT2."}],
 teach:"With an ACE inhibitor, downstream goes down and upstream goes up. Angiotensin II falls, so both AT1 and AT2 activation fall (no agonist), and aldosterone falls. The body makes more renin and angiotensinogen, and angiotensin I builds up. Bradykinin rises because ACE no longer breaks it down.",
 quote:"But also my body is gonna be compensating by trying to upstream, make more renin, angiotensinogen, and you're gonna have this buildup of angiotensin 1, right, that is not going anywhere in this pathway. So downstream goes down, upstream goes up. … It's going down. Because if I don't have the agonist, how am I going to activate the receptors?",
 cite:"PCOL-RAAS_26s.pdf slides 12, 26, 29; transcript 10/8"},

{id:"L12-029", lecture:"L12", prof:"Gottlieb", tier:"new", topic:"L12", sub:"acei", skill:"recall",
 concept:"acei-first-line", tags:[], source:"both",
 stem:"Why are angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE) inhibitors first-line drugs?",
 options:[
  {t:"They decrease mortality and improve quality of life", correct:true, why:"Drugs that save lives are first line; ACE inhibitors decrease mortality and progression of heart disease and improve quality of life."},
  {t:"They are the newest class, with little long-term data", correct:false, why:"That describes aliskiren; ACE inhibitors have been used since the 1960s with a lot of data."},
  {t:"They have no adverse effects", correct:false, why:"They are well tolerated, but cause hyperkalemia, first-dose hypotension, fetal harm, dry cough and angioedema."},
  {t:"They block the ACE enzyme irreversibly", correct:false, why:"They are reversible antagonists."},
  {t:"They act only on the AT2 receptor", correct:false, why:"They lower angiotensin II, so both AT1 and AT2 activation fall."}],
 teach:"ACE inhibitors have been around since the 1960s, with many guidelines and clinical studies behind them, and they are cheap. They decrease mortality and the progression of heart disease and improve quality of life, so they are first line for hypertension, heart failure and other cardiovascular diseases.",
 quote:"So what we're gonna learn in cardio is that drugs that save lives are first line. Because that's what we wanna do. We wanna save our patients' lives for the long term, and we want to improve their quality of life, so they can live a more productive life. And this drug does both, OK?",
 cite:"PCOL-RAAS_26s.pdf slides 9–10; transcript 10/8"},

{id:"L12-030", lecture:"L12", prof:"Gottlieb", tier:"new", topic:"L12", sub:"acei", skill:"recall",
 concept:"acei-prodrugs", tags:[], source:"both", lowYield:true,
 stem:"Which angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE) inhibitor is an active drug rather than a prodrug?",
 options:[
  {t:"Lisinopril", correct:true, why:"Captopril and lisinopril are active; the other ACE inhibitors are prodrugs."},
  {t:"Enalapril", correct:false, why:"A prodrug that must be metabolized to its active form."},
  {t:"Ramipril", correct:false, why:"A prodrug."},
  {t:"Quinapril", correct:false, why:"A prodrug."},
  {t:"Benazepril", correct:false, why:"A prodrug."}],
 teach:"The ACE inhibitors share one mechanism and differ in potency, affinity and pharmacokinetics, including whether they are a drug or a prodrug. Captopril and lisinopril are active compounds; the rest are prodrugs that must be metabolized to the active form. For the exam he needs the mechanism of action, not which one is a prodrug.",
 quote:"Well, perhaps captopril and lisinopril, they are the active, active compounds in this drug, as everybody else is a pro-drug. What does it mean to be a prodrug? You have to be metabolizing to the active ingredient, right? But for our purpose over here, all we need to know is the mechanism of action and what they're gonna do.",
 cite:"PCOL-RAAS_26s.pdf slides 10–11; transcript 10/8"},

{id:"L12-031", lecture:"L12", prof:"Gottlieb", tier:"new", topic:"L12", sub:"acei", skill:"recall",
 concept:"bradykinin-prostaglandins", tags:[], source:"both",
 stem:"Bradykinin that accumulates with an angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE) inhibitor also raises which substances, dilating the kidneys?",
 options:[
  {t:"Prostaglandins", correct:true, why:"Bradykinin increases prostaglandins, which dilate the kidneys."},
  {t:"Angiotensin II", correct:false, why:"Angiotensin II falls with an ACE inhibitor and constricts renal vessels."},
  {t:"Aldosterone", correct:false, why:"Aldosterone falls with an ACE inhibitor."},
  {t:"Angiotensinogen", correct:false, why:"Angiotensinogen is the liver's substrate for renin, not a product of bradykinin."},
  {t:"Norepinephrine", correct:false, why:"Bradykinin's link on the slide is to prostaglandins."}],
 teach:"The second mechanism of ACE inhibitors is more bradykinin, a potent vasodilator. Bradykinin in turn increases prostaglandins, which dilate the kidneys; ACE inhibitors and angiotensin II receptor blockers (ARBs) are renal protective, which matters in patients with diabetes and hypertension.",
 quote:"So, and bradykinin can also increase the levels of prostaglandins, which is gonna dilate your kidneys.",
 cite:"PCOL-RAAS_26s.pdf slide 12; transcript 10/8"},

/* ---------------- ARBs (slides 13–14) ---------------- */
{id:"L12-032", lecture:"L12", prof:"Gottlieb", tier:"new", topic:"L12", sub:"arb", skill:"recall",
 concept:"arb-affinity", tags:[], source:"both",
 stem:"Angiotensin II receptor blockers (ARBs) have about how much higher affinity for AT1 than for AT2?",
 options:[
  {t:"10,000-fold", correct:true, why:"ARBs bind AT1 about 10,000 times more than AT2, so in practice they block only AT1."},
  {t:"10-fold", correct:false, why:"Far too small; the slide gives 10,000-fold."},
  {t:"100-fold", correct:false, why:"Far too small; the slide gives 10,000-fold."},
  {t:"1000-fold", correct:false, why:"1000-fold is the α1 selectivity of prazosin; ARBs are 10,000-fold AT1 over AT2."},
  {t:"Equal affinity", correct:false, why:"They are highly selective for AT1 and have no effect on AT2."}],
 teach:"The ARBs (losartan, valsartan and the other “-sartans”) have about 10,000-fold higher affinity for AT1 than for AT2. They block AT1 and leave AT2 alone; it would take a very large dose to inhibit AT2.",
 quote:"So, when we look, bless you, when we look at the mechanism of action of our RBs, these drugs are 10,000 fold higher affinity for the 81 than 82",
 cite:"PCOL-RAAS_26s.pdf slides 13–14; transcript 10/8"},

{id:"L12-033", lecture:"L12", prof:"Gottlieb", tier:"new", level:1, topic:"L12", sub:"arb", skill:"apply", multi:true,
 concept:"arb-up-down", tags:["in-class"], source:"both",
 stem:"A patient takes losartan. Which changes are expected? Select all that apply.",
 options:[
  {t:"AT1 activation falls", correct:true, why:"Losartan blocks AT1 with high affinity."},
  {t:"AT2 activation rises", correct:true, why:"Angiotensin II, with AT1 blocked, binds AT2 instead."},
  {t:"Angiotensin II levels rise", correct:true, why:"The body compensates upstream and makes more angiotensin II."},
  {t:"Angiotensin I levels rise", correct:true, why:"Upstream components rise as the body compensates."},
  {t:"Angiotensin II levels fall", correct:false, why:"The block is at the receptor, after angiotensin II; its levels rise."},
  {t:"Aldosterone rises", correct:false, why:"With AT1 blocked, aldosterone production goes down."}],
 teach:"An angiotensin II receptor blocker (ARB) blocks the receptor, the last step, so the body compensates by raising everything upstream: renin, angiotensinogen, angiotensin I and angiotensin II. It does not matter, because the block is too strong to outcompete. With AT1 blocked, angiotensin II binds AT2, so AT2 activation goes up and aldosterone goes down.",
 quote:"So if I block the 81, who goes up? … So I already told you 82 goes up. What else goes up? Will angiotensin 2 levels goes up. Yes, because our body is gonna compensate with the AceC goes up and angiotensin 1.",
 note:"He asked the class “So if I block the 81, who goes up?” (81 and 82 are the transcription of AT1 and AT2) and answered it.",
 reading:[{src:"Katzung 16e, Ch. 17", sec:"Angiotensin Receptor Blockers", t:"The available ARBs are selective for the AT1 receptor. Prolonged treatment disinhibits renin release and increases circulating angiotensin II levels, so there may be increased stimulation of AT2 receptors, whose activation causes vasodilation and other beneficial effects."}],
 cite:"PCOL-RAAS_26s.pdf slides 13, 27, 29; transcript 10/8"},

{id:"L12-034", lecture:"L12", prof:"Gottlieb", tier:"new", level:1, topic:"L12", sub:"arb", skill:"tell",
 concept:"arb-at2-bonus", tags:["in-class"], source:"both",
 stem:"An ARB (angiotensin II receptor blocker) leaves the AT2 receptor unblocked. What is the result?",
 options:[
  {t:"More AT2 activation, which opposes AT1", correct:true, why:"Angiotensin II has only AT2 left to bind, and AT2 is the physiological antagonist of AT1."},
  {t:"Blood pressure rises through AT2", correct:false, why:"AT2 (Gi) lowers blood pressure."},
  {t:"AT2 drives vasoconstriction and remodeling", correct:false, why:"Those are AT1 effects; AT2 causes vasodilation and less remodeling."},
  {t:"Bradykinin accumulates and causes cough", correct:false, why:"Bradykinin accumulation belongs to the angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE) inhibitors."},
  {t:"Aldosterone release increases", correct:false, why:"AT2 activation suppresses aldosterone."}],
 teach:"ARBs block AT1, the accelerator, and leave AT2 alone. Angiotensin II, now with less competition, activates AT2, the physiological antagonist that causes vasodilation, less remodeling and more Na+ excretion. So the brakes are applied on top of removing the accelerator.",
 quote:"Furthermore, it leaves the 82 alone. Is that a good thing or a bad thing? It's the cherry on top because that's the physiological antagonist, so you not only inhibit the activation, but now you can start activating the receptor that's gonna further lower everything down, right?",
 note:"He asked the class whether leaving AT2 alone is “a good thing or a bad thing” and answered it.",
 cite:"PCOL-RAAS_26s.pdf slides 13, 18, 26–27; transcript 10/8"},

{id:"L12-036", lecture:"L12", prof:"Gottlieb", tier:"new", topic:"L12", sub:"arb", skill:"recall",
 concept:"losartan-metabolite", tags:[], source:"both", lowYield:true,
 stem:"Which statement about losartan is CORRECT?",
 options:[
  {t:"Its metabolite EXP 3174 also blocks AT1", correct:true, why:"Losartan is active, and its metabolite EXP 3174 is active too, with greater affinity than losartan."},
  {t:"It forms a covalent bond with the AT1 receptor", correct:false, why:"Losartan is reversible; the tight-binding angiotensin II receptor blockers (ARBs) still do not form covalent bonds."},
  {t:"It binds AT2 more than AT1", correct:false, why:"It has about 10,000-fold higher affinity for AT1."},
  {t:"It is inactive until it is metabolized", correct:false, why:"The slide marks losartan as active; its metabolite adds a second phase of AT1 block."},
  {t:"It is the most potent ARB", correct:false, why:"Losartan is last in the rank order of affinity; candesartan is the most potent."}],
 teach:"Losartan is a reversible, selective AT1 antagonist; about 14% of an oral dose is converted to EXP 3174, an active metabolite with greater affinity than losartan, so the effect comes in two phases. It is the least potent ARB in the rank order (candesartan the most), so it may need a higher dose. For the exam he needs only that an ARB is an AT1 receptor antagonist.",
 quote:"Out of these drugs, valsartan is an active drug, and losartan is active, but one of its metabolites is also active, so you get like a double bang, and actually the metabolite that XXP 3174 has a greater affinity than losartan does.",
 note:"One sentence in the audio reads “Losartan is a prodrug and is the most powerful of them all”, apparently a garbled sentence about candesartan; slide 13 marks losartan “Active, but EXP3174” and puts it last in the rank order, and he later said losartan “is an active ingredient, but one of its metabolites is also active”. Keyed to the slides. He also said “I don't care if you know which one is the most potent”, so this item is low yield.",
 cite:"PCOL-RAAS_26s.pdf slides 13–14; transcript 10/8"},

{id:"L12-037", lecture:"L12", prof:"Gottlieb", tier:"new", level:3, ladder:"ace-inhibitor", ladderName:"ACE inhibitor, spironolactone, potassium and the cough", topic:"L12", sub:"arb", skill:"apply",
 concept:"arb-when", tags:[], source:"transcript",
 stem:"Which patient taking lisinopril is the best candidate to switch to an angiotensin II receptor blocker (ARB)?",
 options:[
  {t:"One with a dry cough", correct:true, why:"ARBs are given to patients who are resistant to angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE) inhibitors, have the dry cough, or cannot take an ACE inhibitor."},
  {t:"One with a potassium of 5.5", correct:false, why:"ARBs also cause hyperkalemia; switching does not remove the risk."},
  {t:"One who is now pregnant", correct:false, why:"ARBs, ACE inhibitors and renin inhibitors can all harm the fetus; the drug must be discontinued."},
  {t:"One with first-dose hypotension", correct:false, why:"First-dose hypotension is shared by renin inhibitors, ACE inhibitors and ARBs."},
  {t:"One whose kidney function is compromised and potassium is rising", correct:false, why:"Hyperkalemia in renally compromised patients is shared by all three classes."}],
 teach:"ARBs are typically given to patients who are resistant to ACE inhibitors, who develop the dry cough, or who have a contraindication to an ACE inhibitor; they can also be used first line. Hyperkalemia, first-dose hypotension and fetal harm are shared by all three classes, so switching does not solve them.",
 quote:"And those are gonna be typically given to patients who are either resistant to the ACEs or perhaps have that dry cough or maybe a contraindication to using an ACE inhibitor, OK? So we can use that as a 2nd line, but you could use it as a 1st line too, if you decided to do so.",
 reading:[{src:"Katzung 16e, Ch. 17", sec:"Angiotensin Receptor Blockers", t:"ARBs such as losartan and valsartan are competitive AT1 antagonists whose efficacy in hypertension is similar to that of ACE inhibitors, but they are associated with a lower incidence of cough. They provide a useful alternative when ACE inhibitors are not well tolerated, and should not be used in pregnancy."}],
 cite:"PCOL-RAAS_26s.pdf slides 13, 19; transcript 10/8"},

{id:"L12-038", lecture:"L12", prof:"Gottlieb", tier:"new", level:1, topic:"L12", sub:"arb", skill:"apply", multi:true,
 concept:"losartan-map", tags:["exam-cue"], source:"both",
 stem:"Losartan blocks AT1 receptors (Gq) in the veins and the arteries. Which effects on the mean arterial pressure (MAP) equation follow? Select all that apply.",
 options:[
  {t:"Preload decreases", correct:true, why:"Blocking AT1 in the veins relaxes them, so preload falls."},
  {t:"Afterload decreases", correct:true, why:"Blocking AT1 in the arteries relaxes them, so afterload falls."},
  {t:"Systemic vascular resistance decreases", correct:true, why:"Relaxed arteries lower systemic vascular resistance in the MAP formula."},
  {t:"Preload increases", correct:false, why:"AT1 in the veins constricts; blocking it lowers preload."},
  {t:"Afterload increases", correct:false, why:"AT1 in the arteries constricts; blocking it lowers afterload."}],
 teach:"Apply the drug to the MAP formula. AT1 receptors are in the arteries and the veins and are Gq, so they constrict. Blocking them in the veins decreases preload; blocking them in the arteries decreases afterload and systemic vascular resistance. The same reasoning applies to angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE) inhibitors, renin inhibitors and β blockers.",
 quote:"As such, I know that AT1s are in the arteries. I know that AT1s are in the veins, and those are typically called GGQ, which they're going to constrict. So if I block them by blocking the A1 in the veins, I'm going to do what? Decrease the preload by blocking the A1 in the arteries. I'm going to decrease the afterload and thus decrease my systemic vascular resistance.",
 cite:"PCOL-RAAS_26s.pdf slide 30; transcript 10/8"},

/* ---------------- Angiotensin II actions, remodeling, AT1 vs AT2 (slides 15–18) ---------------- */
{id:"L12-039", lecture:"L12", prof:"Gottlieb", tier:"new", topic:"L12", sub:"ang2", skill:"recall", multi:true,
 concept:"ang2-actions", tags:[], source:"both",
 stem:"Which are actions of angiotensin II? Select all that apply.",
 options:[
  {t:"Direct vasoconstriction", correct:true, why:"Through AT1 (Gq, more Ca++) it constricts vascular smooth muscle."},
  {t:"More Na+ reabsorption in the renal tubules", correct:true, why:"It stimulates the Na+/H+ and Na+/K+/2Cl− transporters on the nephron."},
  {t:"Aldosterone release from the adrenal cortex", correct:true, why:"It stimulates the synthesis and release of aldosterone."},
  {t:"Less renal blood flow", correct:true, why:"It directly constricts renal vascular smooth muscle, which affects GFR."},
  {t:"More renal blood flow", correct:false, why:"Renal blood flow falls as blood is shunted to the main circulation."},
  {t:"Inhibition of aldosterone synthesis", correct:false, why:"Angiotensin II stimulates aldosterone."}],
 teach:"Angiotensin II at AT1 causes direct vasoconstriction (total peripheral resistance up), increases Na+ reabsorption in the renal tubules (Na+/H+ and Na+/K+/2Cl− transporters) with water following sodium, stimulates aldosterone synthesis and release from the adrenal cortex, and constricts renal vascular smooth muscle, decreasing renal blood flow and affecting the glomerular filtration rate (GFR).",
 quote:"In addition to that, we also have 81 on the nephron that can directly increase the reabsorption of sodium, and the more sodium is reabsorbed, the more water is going to follow.",
 reading:[{src:"Katzung 16e, Ch. 17", sec:"Actions of Angiotensin II — Adrenal Cortex & Kidney", t:"Angiotensin II acts directly on the zona glomerulosa of the adrenal cortex to stimulate aldosterone synthesis and release. It acts on the kidney to cause renal vasoconstriction, increase proximal tubular sodium reabsorption and inhibit renin release; a large part of its pressor response is direct contraction of arteriolar smooth muscle."}],
 cite:"PCOL-RAAS_26s.pdf slide 15; transcript 10/8"},

{id:"L12-040", lecture:"L12", prof:"Gottlieb", tier:"new", topic:"L12", sub:"ang2", skill:"figure",
 concept:"ang2-tracing", tags:[], source:"both",
 stem:"Two tracings run together. Right after angiotensin II is injected, blood pressure jumps from about 120 to almost 200 mm Hg, while renal blood flow drops close to zero and then slowly recovers. What do the tracings show?",
 options:[
  {t:"Vasoconstriction that shunts blood away from the kidney", correct:true, why:"Angiotensin II constricts vessels (pressure up) and the renal vessels (renal blood flow down), sending blood to the main circulation."},
  {t:"Renal vasodilation with a rise in the glomerular filtration", correct:false, why:"Renal blood flow falls, so the renal vessels are constricted."},
  {t:"AT2-mediated fall in pressure", correct:false, why:"Pressure rises; that is AT1 (Gq) activation."},
  {t:"Renal blood flow rising with pressure", correct:false, why:"The two move in opposite directions: pressure up, renal blood flow down."},
  {t:"Bradykinin-mediated hypotension", correct:false, why:"Bradykinin dilates; the tracing shows a pressure rise."}],
 teach:"Angiotensin II activates AT1 (Gq), raising Ca++ and constricting blood vessels, so blood pressure rises sharply. At the same time it directly constricts the renal vascular smooth muscle, so renal blood flow drops: blood is shunted to the main circulation.",
 quote:"You went from about 120 to almost 200 in this system. And look what happens to renal blood flow. Man, it's just shutting down, right? We're shunting all the blood to the main circulation.",
 cite:"PCOL-RAAS_26s.pdf slide 15; transcript 10/8"},

{id:"L12-041", lecture:"L12", prof:"Gottlieb", tier:"new", topic:"L12", sub:"ang2", skill:"recall",
 concept:"remodeling", tags:[], source:"both",
 stem:"Long-term activation of the renin–angiotensin–aldosterone system (RAAS) remodels the cardiovascular system. Which change is part of this remodeling?",
 options:[
  {t:"Thicker arteries and ventricles", correct:true, why:"Angiotensin II causes hypertrophy of vascular and cardiac cells and more collagen, so walls thicken and lose elasticity."},
  {t:"More elastic arteries", correct:false, why:"Remodeling leaves less elasticity."},
  {t:"Thinner ventricular walls", correct:false, why:"The ventricles get thicker."},
  {t:"Less collagen in the heart", correct:false, why:"Cardiac fibroblasts make and deposit more collagen."},
  {t:"Lower blood pressure as the vessels adapt", correct:false, why:"Remodeling brings higher blood pressure and more work for the heart."}],
 teach:"Angiotensin II changes the morphology of the cardiovascular system: hypertrophy of vascular and cardiac cells, and more collagen made and deposited by cardiac fibroblasts. Arteries and ventricles get thicker and less elastic, blood pressure rises and the heart works harder; this progression is hard to reverse.",
 quote:"Well, that's bad for the cardiovascular system because what it's going to do is make your arteries thicker, your ventricles thicker. So now your heart has to work even harder to pump blood forward, less elasticity, higher blood pressure, it causes change in the morphology, fibrosis.",
 reading:[{src:"Katzung 16e, Ch. 17", sec:"Actions of Angiotensin II — Cell Growth", t:"Angiotensin II is mitogenic for vascular and cardiac muscle cells and may contribute to cardiac hypertrophy. Inhibition of the renin-angiotensin system slows or prevents remodeling after myocardial infarction that would otherwise lead to heart failure."}],
 cite:"PCOL-RAAS_26s.pdf slides 16–17; transcript 10/8"},

{id:"L12-042", lecture:"L12", prof:"Gottlieb", tier:"new", topic:"L12", sub:"ang2", skill:"recall",
 concept:"at1-calcium", tags:["exam-cue"], source:"both",
 stem:"The AT1 receptor triggers many signals. Which one is the primary factor in smooth muscle constriction?",
 options:[
  {t:"Rise in intracellular Ca++", correct:true, why:"AT1 is Gq; more calcium means more smooth muscle constriction."},
  {t:"Fall in cyclic AMP (cAMP) through Gi", correct:false, why:"That is the AT2 receptor."},
  {t:"Rise in bradykinin", correct:false, why:"Bradykinin rises with angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE) inhibitors and is a vasodilator."},
  {t:"Fall in intracellular Ca++", correct:false, why:"Gq raises calcium."},
  {t:"Fall in aldosterone", correct:false, why:"AT1 raises aldosterone."}],
 teach:"The slide lists many AT1 signals (PI3 kinase, Jak/Stat pathways, tyrosine kinases and others) that drive remodeling, hypertrophy, growth and proliferation. For the exam he expects only one: AT1 increases calcium, the primary factor in constriction; frequent constriction thickens the smooth muscle over time.",
 quote:"Now, I don't expect you to memorize all of those, uh, for our exam too. Actually, I don't expect you to memorize any of them other than increasing calcium, right? Because calcium is gonna be the primary factor.",
 cite:"PCOL-RAAS_26s.pdf slides 17–18; transcript 10/8"},

{id:"L12-043", lecture:"L12", prof:"Gottlieb", tier:"new", topic:"L12", sub:"ang2", skill:"tell",
 concept:"at1-vs-at2", tags:[], source:"both",
 stem:"Which statement contrasting the AT1 and AT2 receptors is CORRECT?",
 options:[
  {t:"AT2 causes vasodilation and Na+ excretion", correct:true, why:"AT2 (Gi, less cyclic AMP (cAMP)) opposes AT1: vasodilation, less remodeling, more Na+ excretion."},
  {t:"AT1 decreases remodeling", correct:false, why:"AT1 increases remodeling; AT2 decreases it."},
  {t:"AT2 increases Na+ reabsorption", correct:false, why:"AT1 increases Na+ reabsorption; AT2 increases excretion."},
  {t:"AT1 is coupled to Gi", correct:false, why:"AT1 is Gq (more Ca++); AT2 is Gi."},
  {t:"AT2 raises Ca++ through Gq and constricts vessels", correct:false, why:"That describes AT1."}],
 teach:"AT1 and AT2 have opposite effects. AT1 (Gq, more Ca++): vasoconstriction, more remodeling, more Na+ reabsorption. AT2 (Gi, less cAMP): vasodilation, less remodeling, more Na+ excretion. One constricts, the other dilates; one retains water, the other loses water.",
 quote:"The alpha 2 is the opposite, but angiotensin 2 doesn't have a lot of affinity for it. So it requires a much higher dose for you to activate those receptors, but because they have opposite effects, we can get a little bonus by blocking that 81 because remember one constrict, the other dilate. One retains water, the other one loses water.",
 note:"In this passage “the alpha 2” is a slip for AT2; the slide is titled “AT1 & AT2 have Opposite Effects”.",
 cite:"PCOL-RAAS_26s.pdf slide 18; transcript 10/8"},

{id:"L12-044", lecture:"L12", prof:"Gottlieb", tier:"new", topic:"L12", sub:"ang2", skill:"apply",
 concept:"anti-remodeling", tags:[], source:"transcript",
 stem:"Which long-term benefit do ACE (angiotensin-converting enzyme) inhibitors and ARBs (angiotensin II receptor blockers) share?",
 options:[
  {t:"Slowing or reversing cardiovascular remodeling", correct:true, why:"Blocking angiotensin II's AT1 signal can slow or reverse remodeling, adding years to a patient's life."},
  {t:"More collagen deposited in the heart by fibroblasts", correct:false, why:"Collagen deposition is part of remodeling, which they reduce."},
  {t:"Thicker resistance arteries", correct:false, why:"Thickening is the remodeling they slow."},
  {t:"Lower serum potassium", correct:false, why:"They can raise potassium (hyperkalemia)."},
  {t:"Stronger AT1 signaling", correct:false, why:"Both reduce AT1 activation."}],
 teach:"Keeping the renin–angiotensin–aldosterone system (RAAS) on all the time remodels the cardiovascular system. ARBs and ACE inhibitors can slow or perhaps reverse that remodeling, so the sooner hypertension is diagnosed and treated, the better the outcome.",
 quote:"So one. One of the benefits of taking an ARRB or ACE inhibitor is that we can slow down or perhaps reverse the remodeling of the cardiovascular system, and that's gonna add ages to your patient's lifetime.",
 cite:"PCOL-RAAS_26s.pdf slides 16–17; transcript 10/8"},

/* ---------------- Adverse effects (slide 19) ---------------- */
{id:"L12-045", lecture:"L12", prof:"Gottlieb", tier:"new", topic:"L12", sub:"adr", skill:"recall", multi:true,
 concept:"shared-adrs", tags:[], source:"both",
 stem:"Which adverse effects are shared by renin inhibitors, ACE (angiotensin-converting enzyme) inhibitors and ARBs (angiotensin II receptor blockers)? Select all that apply.",
 options:[
  {t:"Hyperkalemia", correct:true, why:"All three can cause hyperkalemia, especially in patients with kidney disease."},
  {t:"First-dose hypotension", correct:true, why:"All three can drop blood pressure too far at first, especially in patients highly dependent on the renin–angiotensin–aldosterone system (RAAS)."},
  {t:"Harm to the fetus", correct:true, why:"All three have fetopathic potential; the RAAS is essential for fetal development."},
  {t:"Hypokalemia", correct:false, why:"They raise potassium, not lower it."},
  {t:"Gynecomastia", correct:false, why:"Gynecomastia in men is a spironolactone effect."}],
 teach:"Adverse effects of the renin inhibitor, ACE inhibitors and ARBs are rare and the drugs are well tolerated. All three can cause hyperkalemia (renally compromised patients), first-dose hypotension (start low, go slow) and fetal harm. The ACE inhibitors add dry cough and angioedema through bradykinin.",
 quote:"Typically maybe they might get too much hypotension, so we're gonna start low and go slow, right? Uh, they can get hyperkalemia, OK, so all three can cause hyperkalemia, especially if our patient has kidney disease. … Now, all three drugs can also cause fetus damage.",
 cite:"PCOL-RAAS_26s.pdf slide 19; transcript 10/8"},

{id:"L12-046", lecture:"L12", prof:"Gottlieb", tier:"new", level:1, topic:"L12", sub:"adr", skill:"apply",
 concept:"hyperkalemia-risk", tags:["in-class"], source:"both",
 stem:"An ACE (angiotensin-converting enzyme) inhibitor is about to be started. Which patient is at greatest risk of hyperkalemia?",
 options:[
  {t:"Kidney disease, potassium 5.5", correct:true, why:"Kidney disease and a potassium already above the 3 to 5 range leave no room; the patient enters the hyperkalemic range and risks arrhythmias."},
  {t:"Normal kidneys, potassium 3.5", correct:false, why:"A potassium of 3 or 3.5 leaves plenty of room."},
  {t:"Normal kidneys, potassium 3.0", correct:false, why:"There is a lot of room before hyperkalemia."},
  {t:"Normal kidneys, potassium 4.0", correct:false, why:"Within the 3 to 5 range with healthy kidneys."}],
 teach:"Blood potassium normally runs about 3 to 5. A patient at 3 or 3.5 has room, but one at 5 or 5.5, especially with kidney disease, can be pushed into the hyperkalemic range by an ACE inhibitor or angiotensin II receptor blocker (ARB) and is at higher risk of arrhythmias. Check a baseline potassium before therapy and follow the labs.",
 quote:"What is our, what is our range for potassium in our blood when you do the labs? So about 3 to 5, right? So if your patient has a 3 or 3.5. Give it away. … However, if your patients 5, 5.5, you're starting them on an ACE inhibitor or ARB, that's gonna put them into that hyperkalemic range, which it means is they're higher risk for arrhythmias.",
 note:"He asked the class for the normal potassium range and answered it.",
 cite:"PCOL-RAAS_26s.pdf slide 19; transcript 10/8"},

{id:"L12-047", lecture:"L12", prof:"Gottlieb", tier:"new", topic:"L12", sub:"adr", skill:"recall",
 concept:"acei-cough", tags:["in-class"], source:"both",
 stem:"What causes the dry cough of ACE (angiotensin-converting enzyme) inhibitors?",
 options:[
  {t:"Accumulation of bradykinin", correct:true, why:"ACE normally breaks down bradykinin; with ACE blocked, bradykinin accumulates."},
  {t:"Accumulation of angiotensin II", correct:false, why:"Angiotensin II falls with an ACE inhibitor."},
  {t:"Hyperkalemia", correct:false, why:"Hyperkalemia is a separate adverse effect shared by all three classes."},
  {t:"Excess aldosterone", correct:false, why:"Aldosterone falls with an ACE inhibitor."},
  {t:"Blockade of AT2 in the airways", correct:false, why:"ACE inhibitors do not block receptors; the cough is a bradykinin effect."}],
 teach:"The dry cough is the most common adverse effect of ACE inhibitors: 5 to 20% of patients, usually developing between 1 week and 6 months, from accumulation of bradykinin. In most patients it goes away; those who cannot tolerate it can switch to another class such as an angiotensin II receptor blocker (ARB).",
 quote:"This is also going to be associated with one of its most common and annoying side effects. Do you know what is the most common side effect associated with ACE inhibitors? That dry cough.",
 note:"He asked the class for the most common side effect of ACE inhibitors and answered it.",
 cite:"PCOL-RAAS_26s.pdf slide 19; transcript 10/8"},

{id:"L12-048", lecture:"L12", prof:"Gottlieb", tier:"new", level:3, topic:"L12", sub:"adr", skill:"apply",
 concept:"angioedema-switch", tags:[], source:"both",
 stem:"A patient taking enalapril develops swelling of the lips, face and airway. What is the best next step?",
 options:[
  {t:"Stop enalapril and try an ARB", correct:true, why:"Angioedema is a bradykinin effect of angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE) inhibitors; an ARB (angiotensin II receptor blocker) has no cross-class effect."},
  {t:"Switch to lisinopril", correct:false, why:"Lisinopril is another ACE inhibitor and also raises bradykinin."},
  {t:"Continue; it fades within a month", correct:false, why:"That describes the dry cough; angioedema can close the airway and be life threatening."},
  {t:"Double the enalapril dose", correct:false, why:"More ACE inhibition means more bradykinin."},
  {t:"Add spironolactone", correct:false, why:"Spironolactone does not address angioedema and adds hyperkalemia risk."}],
 teach:"Angioedema is swelling of the lips, airways and face and can be life threatening by closing the airway. It is a bradykinin effect of ACE inhibitors and is about 4 to 4.5 times more prevalent in African Americans. A patient with angioedema on an ACE inhibitor can try an ARB, as there is no cross-class effect.",
 quote:"So angioedema is the swelling of your lips, your airways, your face, so, and that can be life threatening because it can close your airways and you can't breathe. So if you are, uh, if you have angioedema to the ace, you can try the ARB. Because there's no cross, you know, class effect.",
 note:"Slide 19 prints the incidence as “1-.5% of pts”; in lecture he gave only the prevalence ratio (“about 4 times higher in patients who are African Americans”). Neither number is asked.",
 cite:"PCOL-RAAS_26s.pdf slide 19; transcript 10/8"},

{id:"L12-049", lecture:"L12", prof:"Gottlieb", tier:"new", topic:"L12", sub:"adr", skill:"tell", multi:true,
 concept:"acei-specific-adrs", tags:[], source:"both",
 stem:"Which adverse effects are more common with ACE (angiotensin-converting enzyme) inhibitors than with ARBs (angiotensin II receptor blockers), because of bradykinin? Select all that apply.",
 options:[
  {t:"Dry cough", correct:true, why:"From accumulation of bradykinin; more with ACE inhibitors than with ARBs or renin inhibitors."},
  {t:"Angioedema", correct:true, why:"Also a bradykinin effect of the ACE inhibitors."},
  {t:"Hyperkalemia", correct:false, why:"Shared by all three classes."},
  {t:"First-dose hypotension", correct:false, why:"Shared by all three classes."},
  {t:"Fetopathic potential", correct:false, why:"Shared by all three classes."}],
 teach:"Only the ACE inhibitors raise bradykinin, and two adverse effects follow from it: dry cough and angioedema. Hyperkalemia, first-dose hypotension and fetal harm are shared by the renin inhibitor, ACE inhibitors and ARBs.",
 quote:"Now with the ACE inhibitors we have two side effects that seem to be more common associated with it because of the bradykinin. One is the dry cough that is more with the ACEs than you would get with the ARBs or the RAI inhibitors, and the other one is angioedema.",
 note:"The Exam 2 drug list gives angioedema and cough for aliskiren and angioedema for the ARBs as well. Slide 19 lists both under ACE inhibitors only, and he called them more common with ACE inhibitors because of bradykinin. Keyed to the slide and lecture.",
 reading:[{src:"Katzung 16e, Ch. 17", sec:"Inhibition of the Renin-Angiotensin System", t:"Bradykinin is apparently responsible for some adverse effects of ACE inhibitors, including cough and angioedema. ARBs are associated with a lower incidence of cough, and aliskiren does not increase bradykinin levels or cause cough or angioedema."}],
 cite:"PCOL-RAAS_26s.pdf slide 19; transcript 10/8"},

{id:"L12-050", lecture:"L12", prof:"Gottlieb", tier:"new", topic:"L12", sub:"adr", skill:"apply",
 concept:"pregnancy-raas", tags:[], source:"both",
 stem:"A woman taking losartan for hypertension plans to become pregnant. What is the CORRECT action?",
 options:[
  {t:"Discontinue losartan", correct:true, why:"Renin inhibitors, angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE) inhibitors and angiotensin II receptor blockers (ARBs) can all harm the fetus, especially in the first trimester."},
  {t:"Switch to lisinopril", correct:false, why:"ACE inhibitors also have fetopathic potential."},
  {t:"Switch to aliskiren", correct:false, why:"The renin inhibitor also has fetopathic potential."},
  {t:"Continue; only ACE inhibitors harm the fetus", correct:false, why:"All three classes can harm the fetus."},
  {t:"Lower the losartan dose", correct:false, why:"The drug must be discontinued, not reduced."}],
 teach:"All three classes acting on the renin–angiotensin–aldosterone system (RAAS) (renin inhibitor, ACE inhibitors, ARBs) have fetopathic potential, because the system is essential for fetal development. A woman who is pregnant or wants to become pregnant must be taken off them.",
 quote:"So if you are a woman who is pregnant or wants to get pregnant, you have to be discontinued from an ACE inhibitor or ARRB or a RN inhibitor because it can cause harm to the baby, especially during the first trimester.",
 reading:[{src:"Katzung 16e, Ch. 17", sec:"Inhibition of the Renin-Angiotensin System", t:"ACE inhibitors are contraindicated in pregnancy because they cause fetal kidney damage. ARBs should not be used in pregnancy, and aliskiren is also contraindicated in pregnancy."}],
 cite:"PCOL-RAAS_26s.pdf slide 19; transcript 10/8"},

/* ---------------- Aldosterone, spironolactone, eplerenone (slides 20–22) ---------------- */
{id:"L12-052", lecture:"L12", prof:"Gottlieb", tier:"new", topic:"L12", sub:"aldo", skill:"recall",
 concept:"aldosterone-receptor", tags:[], source:"both",
 stem:"On which kind of receptor does aldosterone act?",
 options:[
  {t:"Nuclear (mineralocorticoid) receptor", correct:true, why:"Aldosterone binds the mineralocorticoid receptor (MR), a nuclear receptor that changes messenger RNA to make more pumps and channels."},
  {t:"Gq-coupled AT1 receptor", correct:false, why:"AT1 is angiotensin II's receptor."},
  {t:"Gi-coupled AT2 receptor", correct:false, why:"AT2 is angiotensin II's second receptor."},
  {t:"Gs-coupled β1 receptor on juxtaglomerular cells", correct:false, why:"β1 controls renin release."},
  {t:"Ligand-gated ion channel", correct:false, why:"Aldosterone acts on a nuclear receptor in the collecting duct cells."}],
 teach:"Aldosterone, made in the adrenal gland, binds the mineralocorticoid receptor (MR), a nuclear receptor. It changes messenger RNA so that collecting duct cells make more Na+ channels, K+ channels and Na+/K+-ATPase pumps, retaining Na+ (and water) at the cost of K+.",
 quote:"Aldosterone is going to bind to the immunocord cord receptor, the MR receptor. This is a nuclear receptor, which means it's going to change your messenger RNA formation to code for more pumps and more channels.",
 cite:"PCOL-RAAS_26s.pdf slides 20–21; transcript 10/8"},

{id:"L12-053", lecture:"L12", prof:"Gottlieb", tier:"new", topic:"L12", sub:"aldo", skill:"recall",
 concept:"potassium-wasting", tags:[], source:"both",
 stem:"Aldosterone is called a “potassium-wasting hormone”. Why?",
 options:[
  {t:"It retains Na+ at the cost of losing K+", correct:true, why:"More channels and Na+/K+-ATPase pumps move Na+ back into the circulation and drive K+ out."},
  {t:"It blocks Na+ channels in the collecting duct", correct:false, why:"It makes more Na+ channels, to retain sodium."},
  {t:"It inhibits the Na+/K+-ATPase pump", correct:false, why:"It makes more of these pumps."},
  {t:"It lowers renin release", correct:false, why:"Its potassium effect comes from pumps and channels in the kidney."},
  {t:"It blocks the mineralocorticoid receptor", correct:false, why:"Aldosterone is the agonist at the mineralocorticoid receptor; spironolactone blocks it."}],
 teach:"The more aldosterone, the more channels and Na+/K+-ATPase pumps the kidney cells make. Sodium is retained and potassium is lost, so aldosterone is the potassium-wasting hormone. An MR antagonist does the reverse: Na+ is lost and K+ is spared.",
 quote:"So the more aldosterone you have, the more channels you're going to produce, the more pumps you're going to make, and thus give you the chance to retain more sodium at the cost of losing what potassium. So aldosterone is a potassium wasting hormone.",
 cite:"PCOL-RAAS_26s.pdf slides 20–21; transcript 10/8"},

{id:"L12-054", lecture:"L12", prof:"Gottlieb", tier:"new", topic:"L12", sub:"aldo", skill:"recall",
 concept:"spironolactone-moa", tags:[], source:"both",
 stem:"How does spironolactone spare potassium?",
 options:[
  {t:"It blocks aldosterone at its receptor", correct:true, why:"As an MR antagonist it outcompetes aldosterone, so fewer channels and pumps are made; Na+ cannot be retained and K+ is not lost."},
  {t:"It blocks AT1 receptors in the collecting duct", correct:false, why:"AT1 block is the angiotensin II receptor blockers (ARBs)."},
  {t:"It inhibits renin release from the kidney", correct:false, why:"Less renin release is the β blockers; renin inhibition is aliskiren."},
  {t:"It activates the mineralocorticoid receptor", correct:false, why:"Activating it would waste potassium, like aldosterone."},
  {t:"It makes more Na+/K+-ATPase pumps", correct:false, why:"It suppresses the making of pumps and channels."}],
 teach:"Spironolactone is a mineralocorticoid receptor (MR) antagonist. It outcompetes aldosterone, so the cells cannot make messenger RNA for the channels and pumps. Without enough channels and pumps, Na+ cannot be retained and K+ cannot be lost: a potassium-sparing diuretic.",
 quote:"As such, by taking spironolactone, for example, this is going to be our MR antagonist. I can outcompete aldosterone, block the ability of my cells to make messenger RNA for the channels in the pumps, and I'm going to suppress the channels in the pumps. And if I don't have enough pumps or channels, now I can't retain sodium, and thus I can't lose potassium.",
 cite:"PCOL-RAAS_26s.pdf slide 21; transcript 10/8"},

{id:"L12-055", lecture:"L12", prof:"Gottlieb", tier:"new", topic:"L12", sub:"aldo", skill:"recall",
 concept:"spironolactone-soa", tags:[], source:"both",
 stem:"Where in the nephron do spironolactone and eplerenone act?",
 options:[
  {t:"Late distal tubule and collecting duct", correct:true, why:"The site of action of the aldosterone receptor blockers: the late distal tubule and collecting duct cells."},
  {t:"Proximal tubule", correct:false, why:"Not the site given for the aldosterone receptor blockers."},
  {t:"Granular juxtaglomerular cells", correct:false, why:"These cells release renin; β blockers act there."},
  {t:"Macula densa of the ascending loop of Henle", correct:false, why:"The macula densa senses Na+ Cl− and controls renin release."},
  {t:"Renal afferent arteriole wall", correct:false, why:"That is where the juxtaglomerular cells sit."}],
 teach:"Aldosterone acts on the mineralocorticoid receptor (MR) in the collecting duct cells, where it makes more pumps and channels. Spironolactone and eplerenone block that receptor at the late distal tubule and collecting duct.",
 quote:"Furthermore, we're going to increase the production of aldosterone, which is a nuclear receptor, we talk about nuclear receptors, for example one. It's going to go to the DNA of your collecting duct cells and start to make more pumps and more channels.",
 cite:"PCOL-RAAS_26s.pdf slide 21; transcript 10/8"},

{id:"L12-056", lecture:"L12", prof:"Gottlieb", tier:"new", topic:"L12", sub:"aldo", skill:"recall", multi:true,
 concept:"spironolactone-adrs", tags:[], source:"both",
 stem:"Which are adverse effects of spironolactone? Select all that apply.",
 options:[
  {t:"Hyperkalemia", correct:true, why:"It spares potassium."},
  {t:"Gynecomastia and impotence in men", correct:true, why:"Breast enlargement and lower libido, especially at higher doses."},
  {t:"Diarrhea", correct:true, why:"A gastrointestinal effect common to spironolactone and eplerenone."},
  {t:"Drowsiness", correct:true, why:"A central nervous system effect, with too large a drop in blood pressure."},
  {t:"Hypokalemia", correct:false, why:"It is potassium-sparing; potassium rises."},
  {t:"Dry cough", correct:false, why:"Dry cough is the angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE) inhibitors (bradykinin)."}],
 teach:"Spironolactone spares potassium, so hyperkalemia is expected. In men it can cause gynecomastia (enlarged, tender breasts) and impotence, especially at higher doses. Gastrointestinal effects such as diarrhea are common to spironolactone and eplerenone, and drowsiness can occur with too much of a drop in blood pressure.",
 quote:"Uh, the side effects, these drugs spare potassium, so guess what they're gonna produce? hyperkalemia, too much potassium, right? In men, spironolactone may also cause enlargement of the breast and may become tender and painful, and it may also decrease their libido, so impotence is going to be a possible side effect, especially with the higher doses.",
 cite:"PCOL-RAAS_26s.pdf slide 22; transcript 10/8"},

{id:"L12-057", lecture:"L12", prof:"Gottlieb", tier:"new", topic:"L12", sub:"aldo", skill:"tell",
 concept:"eplerenone-vs-spironolactone", tags:[], source:"both",
 stem:"Which statement distinguishes eplerenone from spironolactone?",
 options:[
  {t:"Eplerenone is more selective for the kidney MR", correct:true, why:"Eplerenone is the most selective for the mineralocorticoid receptor (MR) in the kidney and leaves MRs elsewhere alone, so no gynecomastia."},
  {t:"Eplerenone causes more gynecomastia", correct:false, why:"Gynecomastia and impotence occur with spironolactone, not with eplerenone."},
  {t:"Eplerenone does not cause hyperkalemia", correct:false, why:"Both spare potassium."},
  {t:"Eplerenone inhibits the androgen receptor more strongly", correct:false, why:"Weak androgen receptor and testosterone inhibition is spironolactone's, which is why it causes gynecomastia."},
  {t:"Eplerenone is a renin inhibitor", correct:false, why:"Both are MR (aldosterone) antagonists."}],
 teach:"Both are mineralocorticoid receptor (MR) antagonists and potassium-sparing diuretics, and both can cause hyperkalemia and gastrointestinal effects. Spironolactone is also a weak inhibitor of the androgen receptor and of testosterone synthesis, giving gynecomastia and impotence in men. Eplerenone is the most selective for the kidney MR, so those effects do not occur with it.",
 quote:"perhaps you can take a pleone, which is the most selective MR in the kidney, and leave the MRs in the other places alone, and you don't have to worry about the growth of breast in men, which may be unattractive to some, right?",
 cite:"PCOL-RAAS_26s.pdf slide 22; transcript 10/8"},

{id:"L12-058", lecture:"L12", prof:"Gottlieb", tier:"new", topic:"L12", sub:"aldo", skill:"recall",
 concept:"spironolactone-androgen", tags:[], source:"both",
 stem:"Which property of spironolactone underlies both its use for unwanted facial hair in women and gynecomastia in men?",
 options:[
  {t:"Weak inhibition of androgen receptor and testosterone synthesis", correct:true, why:"It weakly inhibits the androgen receptor and testosterone synthesis: less male hormone effect."},
  {t:"Blockade of the mineralocorticoid receptor in the kidney's collecting duct", correct:false, why:"MR blockade in the kidney gives the potassium-sparing diuretic effect."},
  {t:"Retention of potassium", correct:false, why:"Potassium retention causes hyperkalemia, not hormonal effects."},
  {t:"Inhibition of renin", correct:false, why:"Spironolactone does not inhibit renin."},
  {t:"Blockade of β1 receptors", correct:false, why:"Spironolactone does not act on β1."}],
 teach:"Spironolactone is a weak inhibitor of the androgen receptor and of testosterone synthesis. In women, small doses reduce unwanted hair growth (hirsutism); in men, the same action can cause gynecomastia and impotence. Eplerenone, more selective for the kidney mineralocorticoid receptor, does not do this.",
 quote:"Weak inhibitor of the androgen receptor and of testosterone synthesis … you can take a small doses of spironolactone. It's gonna kinda inhibit the, the, the male hormone.",
 cite:"PCOL-RAAS_26s.pdf slide 22; transcript 10/8"},

{id:"L12-059", lecture:"L12", prof:"Gottlieb", tier:"new", level:2, ladder:"ace-inhibitor", ladderName:"ACE inhibitor, spironolactone, potassium and the cough", topic:"L12", sub:"aldo", skill:"apply",
 concept:"spironolactone-acei-ddi", tags:[], source:"both",
 stem:"Spironolactone is added to lisinopril as adjunct therapy. Which drug–drug interaction is the main concern?",
 options:[
  {t:"Additive hyperkalemia", correct:true, why:"Both drugs cause hyperkalemia, so the effect adds up; the patient's potassium must not already be too high."},
  {t:"Hypokalemia", correct:false, why:"Both drugs raise potassium."},
  {t:"Loss of the lisinopril effect", correct:false, why:"Spironolactone adds to the effect at the end of the cascade."},
  {t:"More angiotensin II at AT1", correct:false, why:"Lisinopril lowers angiotensin II; spironolactone acts on aldosterone's receptor."},
  {t:"Dry cough from spironolactone", correct:false, why:"Dry cough is the angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE) inhibitor's bradykinin effect, not an interaction."}],
 teach:"Spironolactone acts at the end of the cascade and can be added to an ACE inhibitor or angiotensin II receptor blocker (ARB) as adjunct therapy. Both drugs cause hyperkalemia, so the effect can be additive; it is used only if the patient's potassium is not too high, with labs followed.",
 quote:"so it can be used as adjunct therapy to an AC or ARB, assuming their potassium is not too high, because remember, both drugs cause hyperkalemia. We may have an additive effect.",
 cite:"PCOL-RAAS_26s.pdf slides 19, 22, 25; transcript 10/8"},

/* ---------------- Predicting drug effects; β blockers (slides 24–31) ---------------- */
{id:"L12-060", lecture:"L12", prof:"Gottlieb", tier:"new", level:1, topic:"L12", sub:"predict", skill:"apply",
 concept:"metoprolol-renin", tags:["in-class"], source:"both",
 stem:"How does metoprolol affect the renin–angiotensin–aldosterone system (RAAS)?",
 options:[
  {t:"Lowers renin release by blocking β1", correct:true, why:"Blocking β1 on the juxtaglomerular cells means less renin is made."},
  {t:"Abolishes all renin release from the kidney", correct:false, why:"The macula densa (sodium) and baroreceptor controls remain, so renin is suppressed, not abolished."},
  {t:"Inhibits angiotensin-converting enzyme", correct:false, why:"That is the “-prils”."},
  {t:"Blocks AT1 receptors", correct:false, why:"That is the “-sartans”."},
  {t:"Blocks the mineralocorticoid receptor", correct:false, why:"That is spironolactone and eplerenone."}],
 teach:"Metoprolol is a selective β1 antagonist; all β blockers block β1, so all can lower renin production. Blocking β1 on the juxtaglomerular cells suppresses renin but does not abolish it, because the sodium (macula densa) and baroreceptor controls remain.",
 quote:"And by blocking the beta ones in the kidney, you're also gonna decrease renin production, right? You're not gonna abolish because you you have the sodium component and the, the barrel receptor component, but you can suppress it.",
 note:"A student asked “He said we could use a beta blocker. Correct.” and he answered. The Exam 2 drug list says “Less renin production → no RAAS pathway”; he said renin is suppressed, not abolished. Keyed to the lecture.",
 reading:[{src:"Katzung 16e, Ch. 17", sec:"Inhibition of the Renin-Angiotensin System — Drugs That Block Renin Release", t:"Drugs that block the sympathetic nervous system inhibit the release of renin. Propranolol and other β-adrenoceptor-blocking drugs act by blocking the renal β1 receptors that mediate the sympathetic control of renin release."}],
 cite:"PCOL-RAAS_26s.pdf slides 7, 24–25; transcript 10/8"},

{id:"L12-061", lecture:"L12", prof:"Gottlieb", tier:"new", level:1, ladder:"ace-inhibitor", ladderName:"ACE inhibitor, spironolactone, potassium and the cough", topic:"L12", sub:"predict", skill:"tell",
 concept:"which-raises-bradykinin", tags:[], source:"both",
 stem:"Which of these drugs raises bradykinin?",
 options:[
  {t:"Lisinopril", correct:true, why:"angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE) inhibitors stop ACE from breaking down bradykinin."},
  {t:"Losartan", correct:false, why:"Losartan blocks AT1; bradykinin breakdown by ACE is untouched."},
  {t:"Aliskiren", correct:false, why:"Aliskiren blocks renin, before ACE; ACE still breaks down bradykinin."},
  {t:"Metoprolol", correct:false, why:"Metoprolol lowers renin release through β1."},
  {t:"Spironolactone", correct:false, why:"Spironolactone blocks aldosterone at the end of the cascade."}],
 teach:"ACE has two substrates: angiotensin I and bradykinin. Only drugs that block ACE (lisinopril and the other “-prils”) raise bradykinin, which adds vasodilation and causes the dry cough and angioedema. Losartan, aliskiren, metoprolol and spironolactone act elsewhere in the cascade.",
 quote:"What is gonna be the mechanism of action of the ACE inhibitors like lisinopril to block the ACE enzyme? What is it gonna do? It's gonna lower angiotensin 2, and if I don't have angiotensin 2, I can't activate the A1 or the 82, and I'm gonna lower everything downstream from that, right? It can also increase the levels of bradykinin, so that's sort of like the cherry on top.",
 cite:"PCOL-RAAS_26s.pdf slides 26, 29; transcript 10/8"},

{id:"L12-062", lecture:"L12", prof:"Gottlieb", tier:"new", level:1, topic:"L12", sub:"predict", skill:"tell",
 concept:"which-raises-at2", tags:["exam-cue"], source:"both",
 stem:"Which of these drugs increases AT2 receptor activation?",
 options:[
  {t:"Losartan", correct:true, why:"Blocking AT1 leaves angiotensin II only AT2 to bind, so AT2 activation rises."},
  {t:"Lisinopril", correct:false, why:"Less angiotensin II means less activation of both AT1 and AT2."},
  {t:"Aliskiren", correct:false, why:"Everything downstream of renin falls, including AT2 activation."},
  {t:"Metoprolol", correct:false, why:"Less renin means less angiotensin II for both receptors."},
  {t:"Spironolactone", correct:false, why:"Spironolactone acts at aldosterone's receptor, not on angiotensin II's receptors."}],
 teach:"On the prediction table, losartan is the only one of the five that raises AT2 activation: it blocks AT1, and angiotensin II (which rises) binds AT2, the receptor that opposes AT1. Lisinopril and aliskiren lower angiotensin II, so both AT1 and AT2 activation fall.",
 quote:"They block the 81, which blocks the activation of the 81, but enhance the activation of the 82. Why is that good? Because it opposes the 81.",
 cite:"PCOL-RAAS_26s.pdf slides 26–29; transcript 10/8"},

{id:"L12-063", lecture:"L12", prof:"Gottlieb", tier:"new", level:1, topic:"L12", sub:"predict", skill:"tell",
 concept:"ang1-acei-vs-ri", tags:["exam-cue"], source:"both",
 stem:"How do lisinopril and aliskiren differ in their effect on angiotensin I?",
 options:[
  {t:"Lisinopril raises it; aliskiren lowers it", correct:true, why:"Angiotensin I is upstream of angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE) (it builds up with lisinopril) and downstream of renin (it falls with aliskiren)."},
  {t:"Lisinopril lowers it; aliskiren raises it", correct:false, why:"Reversed: ACE inhibitors block after angiotensin I; renin inhibitors block before it."},
  {t:"Both raise it", correct:false, why:"Aliskiren blocks its formation."},
  {t:"Both lower it", correct:false, why:"With lisinopril, angiotensin I has nowhere to go and builds up."},
  {t:"Neither changes it", correct:false, why:"Both change it, in opposite directions."}],
 teach:"Use the rule downstream goes down, upstream goes up. Aliskiren blocks renin, the first step, so angiotensin I falls. Lisinopril blocks ACE, the step after angiotensin I, so angiotensin I builds up. Both lower angiotensin II and aldosterone. He promised 1 to 2 exam questions on these up-or-down predictions.",
 quote:"So what I have done for you over here for you to study for the exam is a little table where you can practice drawing out the cascade and see who increases and who decreases what in the process, because I guarantee you you're gonna have at least 1 to 2 questions on the effects of these drugs in the cascade.",
 cite:"PCOL-RAAS_26s.pdf slides 8, 12, 29; transcript 10/8"},

/* ---------------- Ladder rung (Tier 3): switching away from the ACE inhibitor ---------------- */
{id:"L12-065", lecture:"L12", prof:"Gottlieb", tier:"new", level:3, ladder:"ace-inhibitor", ladderName:"ACE inhibitor, spironolactone, potassium and the cough", topic:"L12", sub:"adr", skill:"apply",
 concept:"ace-to-arb-switch-potassium", tags:["reversal"], source:"both",
 stem:"A patient takes lisinopril with spironolactone as adjunct therapy. He develops a dry cough, and his potassium is 5.4. Lisinopril is switched to losartan. What is expected?",
 options:[
  {t:"The cough eases; the hyperkalemia risk remains", correct:true, why:"The cough comes from bradykinin, which only the ACE inhibitor raises; losartan and spironolactone both still cause hyperkalemia."},
  {t:"The cough eases; potassium returns to normal", correct:false, why:"Angiotensin II receptor blockers (ARBs) also cause hyperkalemia, and spironolactone still spares potassium; the risk stays."},
  {t:"The cough persists; potassium returns to normal", correct:false, why:"Losartan does not raise bradykinin, so the cough should ease; and the potassium risk does not go away."},
  {t:"The cough persists; the hyperkalemia risk remains", correct:false, why:"The hyperkalemia part is right, but the cough is a bradykinin effect of the ACE inhibitor that losartan does not share."},
  {t:"Both resolve; losartan blocks the MR receptor", correct:false, why:"Losartan blocks AT1; the mineralocorticoid receptor (MR) antagonist here is spironolactone, which keeps potassium up."}],
 teach:"Lisinopril blocks the angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE): angiotensin II falls, and bradykinin, which ACE normally breaks down, builds up and causes the dry cough. Spironolactone, an MR antagonist and potassium-sparing diuretic, adds its hyperkalemia to the ACE inhibitor's when given as adjunct therapy. Switching to an ARB such as losartan removes the bradykinin effect, since there is no cross-class effect, but renin inhibitors, ACE inhibitors and ARBs all cause hyperkalemia, so with spironolactone the potassium (normal about 3 to 5) still has to be watched.",
 quote:"it can be used as adjunct therapy to an AC or ARB, assuming their potassium is not too high, because remember, both drugs cause hyperkalemia. We may have an additive effect.",
 note:"On the cough he said: \"One is the dry cough that is more with the ACEs than you would get with the ARBs or the RAI inhibitors\" and \"if you have angioedema to the ace, you can try the ARB. Because there's no cross, you know, class effect.\" (transcript 10/8).",
 cite:"PCOL-RAAS_26s.pdf slides 19, 22, 26–27; transcript 10/8"}
);
